1
00:00:00,275 --> 00:00:04,062
For more than two hundred years, Newton's law of gravity predicted the motion

2
00:00:04,062 --> 00:00:06,550
of the planets with astonishing precision.

3
00:00:06,550 --> 00:00:08,012
But not perfectly.

4
00:00:08,012 --> 00:00:11,975
Mercury's orbit is an ellipse, and the ellipse slowly turns.

5
00:00:11,975 --> 00:00:15,838
Most of that turning is caused by the pull of the other planets, and Newton's

6
00:00:15,838 --> 00:00:17,400
law accounts for it.

7
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:23,925
But a tiny part, forty-three arcseconds per century, refused to be explained.

8
00:00:24,608 --> 00:00:29,596
In November 1915, Albert Einstein explained it, with a new theory of gravity

9
00:00:29,596 --> 00:00:30,933
and this equation.

10
00:00:30,933 --> 00:00:33,758
On the left, the curvature of space and time.

11
00:00:33,758 --> 00:00:36,246
On the right, energy and momentum.

12
00:00:36,246 --> 00:00:37,971
In John Wheeler's words:

13
00:00:37,971 --> 00:00:43,833
spacetime tells matter how to move, and matter tells spacetime how to curve.

14
00:00:44,033 --> 00:00:47,221
In Einstein's theory, gravity isn't a force at all.

15
00:00:47,221 --> 00:00:51,158
Here's the strangest consequence, which we'll derive in this video.

16
00:00:51,158 --> 00:00:54,008
When you throw an apple, nothing pulls it down.

17
00:00:54,008 --> 00:00:57,933
It follows the straightest possible path through spacetime, and the reason

18
00:00:57,933 --> 00:01:02,208
that path curves back to the ground is that time passes more slowly near the

19
00:01:02,208 --> 00:01:04,983
Earth than higher up.

20
00:01:05,142 --> 00:01:09,242
From that equation flow some of the most remarkable predictions in science.

21
00:01:09,242 --> 00:01:11,779
Light bends around massive objects.

22
00:01:11,779 --> 00:01:15,642
Clocks run at different rates at different heights, which your phone's GPS

23
00:01:15,642 --> 00:01:17,779
has to correct for every day.

24
00:01:17,779 --> 00:01:21,367
Black holes, regions from which not even light can escape.

25
00:01:21,367 --> 00:01:26,017
And ripples in spacetime itself, gravitational waves, detected for the first

26
00:01:26,017 --> 00:01:31,542
time in 2015, a century after Einstein predicted them.

27
00:01:32,725 --> 00:01:36,562
In this video we'll derive Einstein's theory from scratch, and the mathematics

28
00:01:36,562 --> 00:01:38,375
will be front and center.

29
00:01:38,375 --> 00:01:40,750
First, why gravity is geometry:

30
00:01:40,750 --> 00:01:45,437
the equivalence principle, clocks, and the idea that falling is maximal aging.

31
00:01:45,437 --> 00:01:47,887
Then the mathematics of curved spaces:

32
00:01:47,887 --> 00:01:52,175
metrics, tensors, Christoffel symbols, the covariant derivative, and the Riemann

33
00:01:52,175 --> 00:01:53,687
curvature tensor.

34
00:01:53,687 --> 00:01:57,462
Then we'll derive Einstein's equation, two different ways, and see what it

35
00:01:57,462 --> 00:01:58,675
means.

36
00:01:58,675 --> 00:02:01,087
And finally, we'll solve it, and test it:

37
00:02:01,087 --> 00:02:05,700
black holes, Mercury's orbit, the bending of light, and gravitational waves.

38
00:02:07,865 --> 00:02:10,928
Let's begin with the theory Einstein had to replace.

39
00:02:10,928 --> 00:02:15,103
In Newton's theory, a mass M pulls on a mass m with a force proportional to

40
00:02:15,103 --> 00:02:18,915
both masses and inversely proportional to the distance squared.

41
00:02:18,915 --> 00:02:21,628
It's cleaner to think of M as creating a field:

42
00:02:21,628 --> 00:02:26,065
at every point in space there's an arrow, the gravitational field g, telling

43
00:02:26,065 --> 00:02:28,890
any object placed there how to accelerate.

44
00:02:28,890 --> 00:02:32,965
And that field is the downhill slope of a single number at each point, the

45
00:02:32,965 --> 00:02:35,740
potential, phi.

46
00:02:35,848 --> 00:02:40,298
The inverse square law has a beautiful consequence, known as Gauss's law.

47
00:02:40,298 --> 00:02:43,998
Surround the mass with any closed surface and add up how much field passes

48
00:02:43,998 --> 00:02:45,611
through it, the flux.

49
00:02:45,611 --> 00:02:49,848
For a sphere, the area grows like r squared while the field weakens like one

50
00:02:49,848 --> 00:02:53,373
over r squared, so the flux doesn't depend on the size.

51
00:02:53,373 --> 00:02:55,423
And it doesn't depend on the shape either:

52
00:02:55,423 --> 00:03:00,211
any surface enclosing the mass catches the same flux, minus four pi G times

53
00:03:00,211 --> 00:03:02,973
the mass inside.

54
00:03:04,140 --> 00:03:08,340
Spread the mass out with a density rho, and Gauss's law turns into a differential

55
00:03:08,340 --> 00:03:09,428
equation.

56
00:03:09,428 --> 00:03:12,928
By the divergence theorem, the flux through the surface is the integral of

57
00:03:12,928 --> 00:03:15,478
the divergence of g over the inside.

58
00:03:15,478 --> 00:03:19,965
Since that holds for every region, however small, the integrands must match:

59
00:03:19,965 --> 00:03:23,265
the divergence of g is minus four pi G rho.

60
00:03:23,265 --> 00:03:27,440
And since g is minus the gradient of phi, we get Poisson's equation:

61
00:03:27,440 --> 00:03:31,273
the Laplacian of phi equals four pi G rho.

62
00:03:31,273 --> 00:03:34,073
This is Newtonian gravity in one line.

63
00:03:34,073 --> 00:03:35,423
Notice its shape:

64
00:03:35,423 --> 00:03:39,661
second derivatives of a field on the left, the density of matter on the right.

65
00:03:39,661 --> 00:03:41,511
Keep that shape in mind.

66
00:03:41,511 --> 00:03:46,423
Einstein's equation will have exactly the same skeleton.

67
00:03:47,948 --> 00:03:50,148
But there's a problem hiding in that equation.

68
00:03:50,148 --> 00:03:51,573
It has no time in it.

69
00:03:51,573 --> 00:03:55,273
If the density rho changes here, the potential phi changes everywhere in the

70
00:03:55,273 --> 00:03:57,273
universe at the same instant.

71
00:03:57,273 --> 00:04:01,111
Draw it on a spacetime diagram, with space across and time up.

72
00:04:01,111 --> 00:04:04,648
Here's the Sun's history, and here's the Earth's, about five hundred light

73
00:04:04,648 --> 00:04:05,911
seconds away.

74
00:04:05,911 --> 00:04:09,861
Imagine, as a thought experiment, that the Sun suddenly vanished.

75
00:04:09,861 --> 00:04:14,073
In Newton's theory, the Earth would feel the change at that very moment.

76
00:04:14,073 --> 00:04:19,473
But in 1905 Einstein's special relativity established that no influence can

77
00:04:19,473 --> 00:04:21,436
travel faster than light:

78
00:04:21,436 --> 00:04:25,448
the news of anything happening at the Sun can't reach us sooner than eight

79
00:04:25,448 --> 00:04:28,373
minutes and nineteen seconds later.

80
00:04:28,373 --> 00:04:31,486
So Newton's gravity can't be the final word.

81
00:04:31,486 --> 00:04:35,598
A relativistic theory of gravity has to be a field theory with time in it,

82
00:04:35,598 --> 00:04:39,898
where changes spread out no faster than light.

83
00:04:40,015 --> 00:04:43,740
The second puzzle is subtler, and it turned out to be the key.

84
00:04:43,740 --> 00:04:46,715
Mass plays two different roles in Newton's theory.

85
00:04:46,715 --> 00:04:49,990
It measures how strongly gravity pulls on an object:

86
00:04:49,990 --> 00:04:51,640
the gravitational mass.

87
00:04:51,640 --> 00:04:54,640
And it measures how hard the object is to accelerate:

88
00:04:54,640 --> 00:04:56,178
the inertial mass.

89
00:04:56,178 --> 00:05:02,240
Put them together, and the acceleration is the ratio of the two times g.

90
00:05:02,382 --> 00:05:05,457
Nothing in Newton's theory says the two must be equal.

91
00:05:05,457 --> 00:05:07,607
But every experiment says they are.

92
00:05:07,607 --> 00:05:11,482
On the Moon, an astronaut dropped a hammer and a feather, and they landed

93
00:05:11,482 --> 00:05:12,569
together.

94
00:05:12,569 --> 00:05:17,282
Torsion balances in Budapest showed the ratio is the same for every material

95
00:05:17,282 --> 00:05:21,282
to a few parts in a billion, and a satellite experiment has now pushed that

96
00:05:21,282 --> 00:05:24,019
to about one part in a million billion.

97
00:05:24,019 --> 00:05:27,840
Everything falls the same way, whatever it's made of.

98
00:05:27,840 --> 00:05:30,190
For Newton, this was a coincidence.

99
00:05:30,190 --> 00:05:32,390
For Einstein, it was a clue.

100
00:05:32,390 --> 00:05:37,153
If every object falls along exactly the same path, regardless of its mass

101
00:05:37,153 --> 00:05:41,728
or composition, then perhaps the path isn't something the object chooses.

102
00:05:41,728 --> 00:05:45,215
Perhaps falling is a property of space and time themselves.

103
00:05:50,945 --> 00:05:53,783
Before gravity, we need the stage it plays out on.

104
00:05:53,783 --> 00:05:58,183
Special relativity, Einstein's theory from 1905, treats space and time as

105
00:05:58,183 --> 00:06:00,445
a single four-dimensional spacetime.

106
00:06:00,445 --> 00:06:04,070
We'll draw it with one space direction across and time going up, with time

107
00:06:04,070 --> 00:06:08,433
multiplied by the speed of light, c, so both axes are measured in meters.

108
00:06:08,433 --> 00:06:09,720
A point is an event:

109
00:06:09,720 --> 00:06:11,333
a place and a moment.

110
00:06:11,333 --> 00:06:14,433
The history of an object is a line, its worldline.

111
00:06:14,433 --> 00:06:16,308
Something at rest goes straight up.

112
00:06:16,308 --> 00:06:18,220
Something moving tilts.

113
00:06:18,220 --> 00:06:22,320
And light moves at forty-five degrees, one meter of distance per meter of

114
00:06:22,320 --> 00:06:24,495
time.

115
00:06:24,962 --> 00:06:29,712
Different observers, moving relative to each other, slice spacetime differently.

116
00:06:29,712 --> 00:06:32,437
Here's the coordinate grid of one observer.

117
00:06:32,437 --> 00:06:35,112
Here's what it looks like for someone moving to the right:

118
00:06:35,112 --> 00:06:39,925
their time axis tilts toward the light ray, and so does their space axis.

119
00:06:39,925 --> 00:06:43,987
They disagree about which events are simultaneous, and about distances and

120
00:06:43,987 --> 00:06:45,645
durations.

121
00:06:45,645 --> 00:06:47,758
But there's one thing they all agree on.

122
00:06:47,758 --> 00:06:51,970
Take two events, and compute minus c delta t squared plus delta x squared,

123
00:06:51,970 --> 00:06:54,933
plus delta y squared plus delta z squared.

124
00:06:54,933 --> 00:06:59,033
This combination, the spacetime interval, is the same for every observer.

125
00:06:59,033 --> 00:07:00,195
Pick an event.

126
00:07:00,195 --> 00:07:04,308
As we change observers, its time and space coordinates change, but the event

127
00:07:04,308 --> 00:07:08,670
only ever slides along this hyperbola, and the interval stays fixed.

128
00:07:08,670 --> 00:07:14,020
It's the spacetime version of distance, with one crucial minus sign.

129
00:07:14,145 --> 00:07:18,508
For two events along the worldline of a clock, the interval has a direct meaning.

130
00:07:18,508 --> 00:07:22,295
Minus the interval, divided by c squared, is the square of the time that clock

131
00:07:22,295 --> 00:07:23,608
measures between them:

132
00:07:23,608 --> 00:07:25,320
its proper time, tau.

133
00:07:25,320 --> 00:07:29,720
For a clock moving at speed v, d tau is d t times the square root of one minus

134
00:07:29,720 --> 00:07:31,558
v squared over c squared.

135
00:07:31,558 --> 00:07:33,433
Moving clocks run slow.

136
00:07:33,433 --> 00:07:37,083
Add it up along the whole worldline, and a clock's reading is simply the length

137
00:07:37,083 --> 00:07:42,745
of its path through spacetime, measured with this minus-sign geometry.

138
00:07:42,904 --> 00:07:45,566
That minus sign has a famous consequence.

139
00:07:45,566 --> 00:07:48,691
Take two events at the same place, ten years apart.

140
00:07:48,691 --> 00:07:52,404
One twin stays home, on the straight worldline between them, and ages ten

141
00:07:52,404 --> 00:07:53,254
years.

142
00:07:53,254 --> 00:07:56,779
The other flies out at eight tenths the speed of light, turns around, and

143
00:07:56,779 --> 00:07:57,916
comes back.

144
00:07:57,916 --> 00:08:02,154
Their path is bent, and along each leg their clock ticks at only six tenths

145
00:08:02,154 --> 00:08:03,016
the rate.

146
00:08:03,016 --> 00:08:05,962
They return having aged six years.

147
00:08:05,962 --> 00:08:09,662
Every bent path between the same two events ages less, and the faster the

148
00:08:09,662 --> 00:08:10,950
trip, the less.

149
00:08:10,950 --> 00:08:14,550
In ordinary geometry, a straight line is the shortest path.

150
00:08:14,550 --> 00:08:18,775
In spacetime, because of the minus sign, a straight worldline is the longest:

151
00:08:18,775 --> 00:08:20,562
it has the most proper time.

152
00:08:20,562 --> 00:08:23,587
And straight worldlines are what free particles follow.

153
00:08:23,587 --> 00:08:27,525
So here's a law of motion that will survive into general relativity:

154
00:08:27,525 --> 00:08:31,150
a free particle moves so as to maximize its proper time.

155
00:08:31,150 --> 00:08:36,137
Keep it in mind, because it's how gravity is about to sneak in.

156
00:08:36,295 --> 00:08:39,483
Let's write the interval in the notation we'll use from now on.

157
00:08:39,483 --> 00:08:43,683
Label the four coordinates x zero through x three, with x zero equal to c

158
00:08:43,683 --> 00:08:44,008
t.

159
00:08:44,008 --> 00:08:48,670
Then d s squared is eta mu nu, d x mu, d x nu, where eta is this diagonal

160
00:08:48,670 --> 00:08:49,708
matrix:

161
00:08:49,708 --> 00:08:53,095
minus one for time, plus one for each direction of space.

162
00:08:53,095 --> 00:08:55,645
And we use Einstein's summation convention:

163
00:08:55,645 --> 00:08:59,270
whenever an index appears once up and once down, it's summed over all four

164
00:08:59,270 --> 00:09:00,370
values.

165
00:09:00,370 --> 00:09:04,520
Sixteen terms, most of them zero, written in one line.

166
00:09:04,520 --> 00:09:07,020
Eta is the metric of flat spacetime:

167
00:09:07,020 --> 00:09:09,358
the same matrix at every point.

168
00:09:09,358 --> 00:09:11,758
Here's the idea we are building toward:

169
00:09:11,758 --> 00:09:16,483
gravity is what happens when this matrix is replaced by a metric g mu nu that

170
00:09:16,483 --> 00:09:18,195
varies from place to place.

171
00:09:19,835 --> 00:09:25,160
In 1907, two years after special relativity, Einstein was working at the patent

172
00:09:25,160 --> 00:09:28,860
office in Bern when he had what he later called the happiest thought of his

173
00:09:28,860 --> 00:09:29,872
life.

174
00:09:29,872 --> 00:09:34,397
A person falling freely from the roof of a house does not feel their own weight.

175
00:09:34,397 --> 00:09:38,722
For them, at least in their immediate surroundings, there is no gravitational

176
00:09:38,722 --> 00:09:40,860
field.

177
00:09:41,035 --> 00:09:42,347
Turn that around.

178
00:09:42,347 --> 00:09:46,110
Here's a closed box resting on the Earth, and here's an identical box on a

179
00:09:46,110 --> 00:09:50,785
rocket in deep space, far from any planet, accelerating upward at nine point

180
00:09:50,785 --> 00:09:52,872
eight meters per second squared.

181
00:09:52,872 --> 00:09:54,597
Release a ball in each.

182
00:09:54,597 --> 00:09:58,310
In both, the ball falls to the floor in exactly the same way:

183
00:09:58,310 --> 00:10:02,097
on Earth because gravity pulls it, in the rocket because the floor rushes

184
00:10:02,097 --> 00:10:03,310
up to meet it.

185
00:10:03,310 --> 00:10:07,076
No experiment inside the box can tell the difference.

186
00:10:07,076 --> 00:10:08,914
It works the other way too.

187
00:10:08,914 --> 00:10:12,976
Cut the cable, and a box falling freely near the Earth is indistinguishable

188
00:10:12,976 --> 00:10:15,164
from one floating in empty space:

189
00:10:15,164 --> 00:10:17,351
inside, everything floats.

190
00:10:17,351 --> 00:10:19,514
This is the equivalence principle.

191
00:10:19,514 --> 00:10:23,251
Gravity, at least locally, can be made to vanish by falling.

192
00:10:23,251 --> 00:10:25,776
And that's why everything falls the same way:

193
00:10:25,776 --> 00:10:30,001
in the falling box, nothing is falling at all.

194
00:10:30,185 --> 00:10:32,985
Einstein immediately drew two consequences.

195
00:10:32,985 --> 00:10:34,510
The first is about light.

196
00:10:34,510 --> 00:10:37,160
Shine a beam across the accelerating rocket.

197
00:10:37,160 --> 00:10:40,822
Seen from outside, the light travels in a perfectly straight line, but while

198
00:10:40,822 --> 00:10:43,710
it crosses, the cabin speeds up underneath it.

199
00:10:43,710 --> 00:10:47,947
So seen from inside, the beam enters high and hits the far wall lower:

200
00:10:47,947 --> 00:10:51,085
it curves downward, exactly like a thrown ball.

201
00:10:51,085 --> 00:10:54,585
By the equivalence principle, the same must happen on Earth.

202
00:10:54,585 --> 00:10:56,301
Light falls.

203
00:10:56,301 --> 00:10:59,039
On Earth the effect is absurdly small.

204
00:10:59,039 --> 00:11:03,664
Across a ten meter room, light drops by about five femtometers, the width

205
00:11:03,664 --> 00:11:05,151
of three protons.

206
00:11:05,151 --> 00:11:09,851
But near the Sun, as we'll see, it becomes measurable.

207
00:11:09,976 --> 00:11:12,164
The second consequence is about time.

208
00:11:12,164 --> 00:11:16,401
Here's the rocket on a spacetime diagram, with height across and time up.

209
00:11:16,401 --> 00:11:20,664
Because it accelerates, its floor and ceiling trace out curves that bend toward

210
00:11:20,664 --> 00:11:21,964
the speed of light.

211
00:11:21,964 --> 00:11:25,789
Now let a clock on the floor send a flash of light upward at every tick.

212
00:11:25,789 --> 00:11:29,601
Each flash has to chase a ceiling that's moving faster than when the previous

213
00:11:29,601 --> 00:11:30,676
one left.

214
00:11:30,676 --> 00:11:32,751
So the flashes arrive spread out:

215
00:11:32,751 --> 00:11:36,535
the person at the top sees the floor clock ticking slowly.

216
00:11:36,535 --> 00:11:40,460
For a rocket of height h with acceleration g, the ticks arrive stretched by

217
00:11:40,460 --> 00:11:43,610
a factor of one plus g h over c squared.

218
00:11:43,610 --> 00:11:47,672
And by the equivalence principle, the same must be true in a gravitational

219
00:11:47,672 --> 00:11:48,647
field.

220
00:11:48,647 --> 00:11:53,260
A clock lower down, deeper in the potential, really does run slower than one

221
00:11:53,260 --> 00:11:54,285
higher up.

222
00:11:54,285 --> 00:11:58,610
Its rate is one plus phi over c squared.

223
00:11:58,768 --> 00:12:02,530
This was tested in 1959, in a tower at Harvard.

224
00:12:02,530 --> 00:12:06,593
Robert Pound and Glen Rebka sent gamma rays up and down twenty-two and a half

225
00:12:06,593 --> 00:12:07,593
meters.

226
00:12:07,593 --> 00:12:11,330
The predicted shift in frequency is g h over c squared:

227
00:12:11,330 --> 00:12:14,493
two and a half parts in a thousand million million.

228
00:12:14,493 --> 00:12:18,680
Using an exquisitely sharp nuclear resonance, they measured it, and found

229
00:12:18,680 --> 00:12:23,993
agreement within ten percent, later improved to one percent.

230
00:12:24,135 --> 00:12:26,047
And you rely on it every day.

231
00:12:26,047 --> 00:12:30,422
A GPS satellite orbits about twenty-six thousand kilometers from Earth's center,

232
00:12:30,422 --> 00:12:33,885
high up in the potential, so its clock runs faster than yours:

233
00:12:33,885 --> 00:12:36,410
by about forty-six microseconds a day.

234
00:12:36,410 --> 00:12:40,497
It also moves at almost four kilometers per second, so special relativity

235
00:12:40,497 --> 00:12:43,697
slows it, by about seven microseconds a day.

236
00:12:43,697 --> 00:12:44,810
The net effect:

237
00:12:44,810 --> 00:12:48,585
the satellite clocks gain about thirty-eight microseconds every day.

238
00:12:48,585 --> 00:12:51,885
Light travels eleven kilometers in that time.

239
00:12:51,885 --> 00:12:56,010
Without general relativity built into the system, your position would drift

240
00:12:56,010 --> 00:12:57,810
by kilometers a day.

241
00:12:57,810 --> 00:13:02,685
The clocks are deliberately tuned slow before launch.

242
00:13:02,835 --> 00:13:04,647
Let's write that as geometry.

243
00:13:04,647 --> 00:13:08,385
A clock at rest where the potential is phi ticks at rate one plus phi over

244
00:13:08,385 --> 00:13:09,460
c squared.

245
00:13:09,460 --> 00:13:10,997
Squaring, to first order:

246
00:13:10,997 --> 00:13:15,485
c squared d tau squared is one plus two phi over c squared, times c squared

247
00:13:15,485 --> 00:13:16,697
d t squared.

248
00:13:16,697 --> 00:13:19,785
So the spacetime interval near a mass looks like this.

249
00:13:19,785 --> 00:13:22,460
Newton's potential has become part of the metric:

250
00:13:22,460 --> 00:13:25,372
the time-time component, g zero zero.

251
00:13:25,372 --> 00:13:28,560
Space, we'll find later, is warped too.

252
00:13:28,560 --> 00:13:32,560
But for anything moving slowly, this is the part that matters, and in the

253
00:13:32,560 --> 00:13:36,010
next chapter we'll see that it is all of Newtonian gravity.

254
00:13:37,607 --> 00:13:39,182
We now have two facts.

255
00:13:39,182 --> 00:13:43,457
First, free objects follow worldlines of maximal proper time.

256
00:13:43,457 --> 00:13:47,244
Second, near the Earth, clocks run faster the higher they are.

257
00:13:47,244 --> 00:13:51,057
Let's put them together and see what happens.

258
00:13:51,223 --> 00:13:54,948
Throw a ball straight up, and catch it two seconds later at the same spot.

259
00:13:54,948 --> 00:13:58,673
On a spacetime diagram, with time across and height up, that's two events:

260
00:13:58,673 --> 00:14:00,248
the throw and the catch.

261
00:14:00,248 --> 00:14:03,036
And the background is shaded by how fast clocks run:

262
00:14:03,036 --> 00:14:04,623
faster the higher you go.

263
00:14:04,623 --> 00:14:05,423
Now ask:

264
00:14:05,423 --> 00:14:08,648
of all the worldlines that connect these two events, which one has the most

265
00:14:08,648 --> 00:14:09,773
proper time?

266
00:14:09,773 --> 00:14:11,998
Staying on the ground is one option.

267
00:14:11,998 --> 00:14:14,161
We'll measure every other path against it:

268
00:14:14,161 --> 00:14:18,765
tau minus T, the extra time a clock riding along would record.

269
00:14:18,765 --> 00:14:22,602
Go a little higher, and the clock spends its time where time runs faster:

270
00:14:22,602 --> 00:14:23,752
it gains.

271
00:14:23,752 --> 00:14:26,965
Go higher still and it gains more, up to a point.

272
00:14:26,965 --> 00:14:30,815
But to get high and come back in two seconds, the ball must move fast, and

273
00:14:30,815 --> 00:14:32,727
moving clocks run slow.

274
00:14:32,727 --> 00:14:36,127
Go too high, and the speed costs more than the height gains:

275
00:14:36,127 --> 00:14:39,790
at twelve meters, the clock actually loses time.

276
00:14:39,790 --> 00:14:42,190
Somewhere in between there's a best height.

277
00:14:42,190 --> 00:14:46,127
It comes out at four point nine meters, where the ball's clock gains about

278
00:14:46,127 --> 00:14:49,715
three and a half times ten to the minus sixteen seconds.

279
00:14:49,715 --> 00:14:54,477
And four point nine meters is exactly how high Newton says a ball rises if

280
00:14:54,477 --> 00:14:56,602
it's in the air for two seconds.

281
00:14:56,602 --> 00:15:01,665
The path of maximal proper time is the path the ball actually takes.

282
00:15:01,665 --> 00:15:03,177
Here's the bookkeeping.

283
00:15:03,177 --> 00:15:07,565
To first order, the extra time is the integral of g z over c squared, the

284
00:15:07,565 --> 00:15:11,840
gain from height, which grows in proportion to how high you go, minus the

285
00:15:11,840 --> 00:15:16,165
integral of half v squared over c squared, the cost of speed, which grows

286
00:15:16,165 --> 00:15:17,977
like the square of the height.

287
00:15:17,977 --> 00:15:22,265
A gain that grows linearly, minus a cost that grows quadratically:

288
00:15:22,265 --> 00:15:25,140
their difference has a single maximum.

289
00:15:25,140 --> 00:15:26,952
It isn't just parabolas.

290
00:15:26,952 --> 00:15:30,752
Any other way of getting from the throw to the catch, hovering at the top,

291
00:15:30,752 --> 00:15:36,252
rising slowly and falling fast, or going too high, records less proper time.

292
00:15:36,252 --> 00:15:41,990
Among all possible paths, the ball's real one is the longest-lived.

293
00:15:42,182 --> 00:15:43,982
Now let's prove it in general.

294
00:15:43,982 --> 00:15:47,907
The proper time of any path is the integral of d tau, the square root of minus

295
00:15:47,907 --> 00:15:51,569
d s squared over c squared, using the metric we just found:

296
00:15:51,569 --> 00:15:55,794
one plus two phi over c squared, minus v squared over c squared, all under

297
00:15:55,794 --> 00:15:56,744
the root.

298
00:15:56,744 --> 00:16:00,769
Both corrections are tiny, so expand the square root to first order.

299
00:16:00,769 --> 00:16:03,369
Now multiply by minus m c squared.

300
00:16:03,369 --> 00:16:07,244
What's inside the integral is one half m v squared minus m phi:

301
00:16:07,244 --> 00:16:10,307
kinetic energy minus potential energy.

302
00:16:10,307 --> 00:16:14,873
That's exactly the action of Newtonian mechanics, plus a constant.

303
00:16:14,873 --> 00:16:18,048
And the principle of least action says Newton's laws are what you get when

304
00:16:18,048 --> 00:16:19,961
that action is stationary.

305
00:16:19,961 --> 00:16:24,186
So making the proper time stationary gives m times acceleration equals minus

306
00:16:24,186 --> 00:16:26,148
m times the gradient of phi:

307
00:16:26,148 --> 00:16:28,073
Newton's law of gravity.

308
00:16:28,073 --> 00:16:32,173
Notice that the mass cancels from both sides, so every object falls the same

309
00:16:32,173 --> 00:16:33,011
way.

310
00:16:33,011 --> 00:16:34,923
That isn't a coincidence anymore:

311
00:16:34,923 --> 00:16:37,511
the path depends only on the geometry.

312
00:16:37,511 --> 00:16:42,923
Gravity, for slow objects, is nothing but the warping of time.

313
00:16:43,065 --> 00:16:47,265
This is worth pausing on, because you may have seen general relativity pictured

314
00:16:47,265 --> 00:16:50,202
as a ball sitting in a stretched rubber sheet.

315
00:16:50,202 --> 00:16:52,865
That picture is misleading in two ways.

316
00:16:52,865 --> 00:16:55,890
First, it explains gravity using gravity:

317
00:16:55,890 --> 00:16:59,177
the marble rolls down the dip because something pulls it down.

318
00:16:59,177 --> 00:17:01,765
Second, it shows curved space.

319
00:17:01,765 --> 00:17:04,865
But what makes an apple fall is curved time.

320
00:17:04,865 --> 00:17:09,602
Space is warped too, but for slow objects its effect is smaller by a factor

321
00:17:09,602 --> 00:17:11,677
of v squared over c squared:

322
00:17:11,677 --> 00:17:16,340
about ten to the minus fifteen, for a thrown ball.

323
00:17:16,490 --> 00:17:17,702
One more step.

324
00:17:17,702 --> 00:17:21,502
In a rocket, the same speeding up of clocks with height appears in perfectly

325
00:17:21,502 --> 00:17:26,577
flat spacetime, so it can't be the whole story of a real gravitational field.

326
00:17:26,577 --> 00:17:29,127
What can a falling observer never get rid of?

327
00:17:29,127 --> 00:17:32,602
Release a ring of free particles high above the Earth.

328
00:17:32,602 --> 00:17:36,315
Each one falls toward the center of the Earth, along a slightly different

329
00:17:36,315 --> 00:17:40,840
line, and the near side is pulled a little harder than the far side.

330
00:17:40,840 --> 00:17:45,590
From the ring's own falling center, there's no gravity at all, except this:

331
00:17:45,590 --> 00:17:51,115
the ring stretches along the direction of the fall and squeezes sideways.

332
00:17:52,323 --> 00:17:55,961
These are tides, the same effect that raises the oceans.

333
00:17:55,961 --> 00:18:00,348
A freely falling observer can cancel gravity at one point, but not its variation

334
00:18:00,348 --> 00:18:01,686
from point to point:

335
00:18:01,686 --> 00:18:04,761
nearby free paths converge or spread apart.

336
00:18:04,761 --> 00:18:08,048
That's the true, unremovable signature of gravity.

337
00:18:08,048 --> 00:18:12,248
And in geometry, the way that nearby straight lines converge or spread apart

338
00:18:12,248 --> 00:18:13,236
has a name:

339
00:18:13,236 --> 00:18:14,311
curvature.

340
00:18:14,311 --> 00:18:17,948
To say what that means for spacetime, we need some mathematics.

341
00:18:23,170 --> 00:18:26,920
To describe curved spacetime, we need a way to talk about curvature that doesn't

342
00:18:26,920 --> 00:18:28,820
rely on stepping outside.

343
00:18:28,820 --> 00:18:32,907
For surfaces, Carl Friedrich Gauss found one in 1827.

344
00:18:32,907 --> 00:18:34,570
Picture an ant on a sphere.

345
00:18:34,570 --> 00:18:38,007
It can't leave the surface, and it can't see the third dimension.

346
00:18:38,007 --> 00:18:41,807
All it can do is measure distances and angles along the surface.

347
00:18:41,807 --> 00:18:43,795
Gauss proved that this is enough:

348
00:18:43,795 --> 00:18:47,970
the curvature of a surface is determined entirely by measurements made inside

349
00:18:47,970 --> 00:18:48,570
it.

350
00:18:48,570 --> 00:18:52,195
He was so pleased that he called it the remarkable theorem.

351
00:18:52,195 --> 00:18:57,470
Spacetime is the same situation, one dimension up, and with time thrown in.

352
00:18:57,470 --> 00:18:58,895
We live inside it.

353
00:18:58,895 --> 00:19:00,782
There's no outside to look from.

354
00:19:00,782 --> 00:19:06,270
So everything we build from now on will be built from measurements made within.

355
00:19:06,403 --> 00:19:08,665
The basic measurement is distance.

356
00:19:08,665 --> 00:19:12,415
Label the points of a surface with coordinates, and the distance between two

357
00:19:12,415 --> 00:19:15,578
nearby points is given by a formula like this:

358
00:19:15,578 --> 00:19:21,653
d s squared equals g i j, d x i, d x j, summed over i and j.

359
00:19:21,653 --> 00:19:24,940
The coefficients g i j are called the metric.

360
00:19:24,940 --> 00:19:28,795
They convert steps in the coordinates into real distances.

361
00:19:28,795 --> 00:19:32,282
On a flat plane in ordinary coordinates, it's Pythagoras:

362
00:19:32,282 --> 00:19:34,720
d x squared plus d y squared.

363
00:19:34,720 --> 00:19:38,820
The same plane in polar coordinates gives d r squared plus r squared d phi

364
00:19:38,820 --> 00:19:43,170
squared, because a step in angle covers more ground far from the center.

365
00:19:43,170 --> 00:19:47,095
And on a sphere of radius R, with theta measured down from the north pole

366
00:19:47,095 --> 00:19:51,495
and phi around the equator, a step in phi covers less and less ground as you

367
00:19:51,495 --> 00:19:52,832
approach a pole:

368
00:19:52,832 --> 00:19:56,120
that's the sine squared theta.

369
00:19:56,695 --> 00:19:58,420
Here's a way to see a metric.

370
00:19:58,420 --> 00:20:02,407
On the globe, draw little circles, every point at the same small distance

371
00:20:02,407 --> 00:20:04,145
epsilon from a center.

372
00:20:04,145 --> 00:20:05,870
They're all the same size.

373
00:20:05,870 --> 00:20:10,357
Now flatten the globe onto a map, with longitude across and latitude up.

374
00:20:10,357 --> 00:20:15,007
In map coordinates, those identical circles become ellipses, wider and wider

375
00:20:15,007 --> 00:20:19,020
toward the poles, because near a pole you need a big change in longitude to

376
00:20:19,020 --> 00:20:21,195
travel a short distance.

377
00:20:21,195 --> 00:20:23,482
That field of ellipses is the metric:

378
00:20:23,482 --> 00:20:28,536
at every point, it tells you which coordinate steps have the same real length.

379
00:20:28,536 --> 00:20:29,599
But careful.

380
00:20:29,599 --> 00:20:33,936
A metric that varies from place to place doesn't, by itself, mean the space

381
00:20:33,936 --> 00:20:35,136
is curved.

382
00:20:35,136 --> 00:20:39,436
The polar-coordinate metric varies too, and it describes a perfectly flat

383
00:20:39,436 --> 00:20:40,374
plane.

384
00:20:40,374 --> 00:20:43,649
Coordinates can make a flat space look complicated.

385
00:20:43,649 --> 00:20:49,336
So we need a test for curvature that doesn't care which coordinates we use.

386
00:20:49,495 --> 00:20:50,807
Here's the first test:

387
00:20:50,807 --> 00:20:51,970
triangles.

388
00:20:51,970 --> 00:20:55,232
Start at the north pole and walk straight down to the equator.

389
00:20:55,232 --> 00:20:58,882
Turn ninety degrees and walk a quarter of the way around the equator.

390
00:20:58,882 --> 00:21:02,382
Turn ninety degrees again and walk straight back to the pole.

391
00:21:02,382 --> 00:21:05,857
Every side is a straight line, as far as the ant can tell:

392
00:21:05,857 --> 00:21:07,770
it never turned while walking.

393
00:21:07,770 --> 00:21:12,336
And when it arrives, it meets its starting path at another right angle.

394
00:21:12,336 --> 00:21:16,236
The angles add up to two hundred and seventy degrees, not one eighty.

395
00:21:16,236 --> 00:21:18,086
And the excess isn't random.

396
00:21:18,086 --> 00:21:21,236
It equals the triangle's area divided by R squared:

397
00:21:21,236 --> 00:21:25,086
this triangle covers one eighth of the sphere, and one eighth of four pi R

398
00:21:25,086 --> 00:21:28,149
squared, over R squared, is pi over two:

399
00:21:28,149 --> 00:21:30,174
exactly ninety degrees.

400
00:21:30,174 --> 00:21:34,661
In general, the angle excess is the total curvature inside the triangle.

401
00:21:34,661 --> 00:21:40,386
The ant can measure angles and areas, so the ant can measure curvature.

402
00:21:40,528 --> 00:21:43,915
Here's a second test, and it's the one we'll turn into a machine.

403
00:21:43,915 --> 00:21:46,265
Give the ant an arrow to carry, and a rule:

404
00:21:46,265 --> 00:21:48,715
never rotate it relative to the surface.

405
00:21:48,715 --> 00:21:52,390
Walking along a straight line, that means the arrow keeps a fixed angle to

406
00:21:52,390 --> 00:21:53,878
the direction of travel.

407
00:21:53,878 --> 00:21:55,765
Watch it go around the triangle.

408
00:21:55,765 --> 00:21:58,440
Down the first side, pointing along the path.

409
00:21:58,440 --> 00:22:02,290
Along the equator, it now points sideways, because the path turned but the

410
00:22:02,290 --> 00:22:03,415
arrow didn't.

411
00:22:03,415 --> 00:22:06,428
Then back up to the pole.

412
00:22:07,095 --> 00:22:09,982
The arrow comes home rotated by ninety degrees:

413
00:22:09,982 --> 00:22:12,120
exactly the angle excess.

414
00:22:12,120 --> 00:22:16,770
On a flat page, an arrow carried around any loop this way comes back unchanged.

415
00:22:16,770 --> 00:22:20,920
On a curved surface, it comes back rotated by the curvature enclosed.

416
00:22:20,920 --> 00:22:23,220
This is the idea we'll make precise.

417
00:22:23,220 --> 00:22:27,832
The rotation per unit area, at every point and in every orientation, packaged

418
00:22:27,832 --> 00:22:30,620
into one object computed from the metric alone:

419
00:22:30,620 --> 00:22:32,582
the Riemann curvature tensor.

420
00:22:32,582 --> 00:22:35,370
To build it, we first need the language of tensors.

421
00:22:37,050 --> 00:22:40,312
In curved spacetime there are no preferred coordinates.

422
00:22:40,312 --> 00:22:44,475
Any labeling of events is as good as any other, as long as it's smooth.

423
00:22:44,475 --> 00:22:47,287
But the physics can't depend on our choice of labels.

424
00:22:47,287 --> 00:22:50,950
So the laws of physics have to be written as tensor equations:

425
00:22:50,950 --> 00:22:54,512
statements whose truth doesn't depend on the coordinate system.

426
00:22:54,512 --> 00:22:59,875
Let's see what that means, starting with the simplest tensor of all.

427
00:23:00,025 --> 00:23:02,062
A vector is an arrow at a point:

428
00:23:02,062 --> 00:23:04,762
a direction and a size, like a velocity.

429
00:23:04,762 --> 00:23:08,112
To describe it with numbers, we lay down coordinates.

430
00:23:08,112 --> 00:23:12,837
Their grid lines give two basis vectors at our point, e one and e two, and

431
00:23:12,837 --> 00:23:14,612
the vector is some amount of each:

432
00:23:14,612 --> 00:23:17,908
its components, V one and V two.

433
00:23:17,908 --> 00:23:19,783
Now change the coordinates.

434
00:23:19,783 --> 00:23:21,558
The arrow itself doesn't move:

435
00:23:21,558 --> 00:23:23,333
it's the same physical thing.

436
00:23:23,333 --> 00:23:26,858
But the basis vectors change, so the components change.

437
00:23:26,858 --> 00:23:28,496
Now polar coordinates:

438
00:23:28,496 --> 00:23:31,696
new basis vectors, new numbers, same arrow.

439
00:23:31,696 --> 00:23:36,008
How the numbers change is completely determined by how the coordinates change:

440
00:23:36,008 --> 00:23:39,908
the new components are the Jacobian matrix of partial derivatives times the

441
00:23:39,908 --> 00:23:41,021
old ones.

442
00:23:41,021 --> 00:23:44,725
Anything whose components change this way is a vector.

443
00:23:44,725 --> 00:23:46,687
There's a slick way to see why.

444
00:23:46,687 --> 00:23:51,525
Think of the basis vector e mu as the operation "move along the x mu coordinate

445
00:23:51,525 --> 00:23:56,087
line", that is, the partial derivative with respect to x mu.

446
00:23:56,087 --> 00:23:58,962
Then a vector is V mu times partial mu:

447
00:23:58,962 --> 00:24:00,737
a directional derivative.

448
00:24:00,737 --> 00:24:07,050
The chain rule for partial derivatives is exactly the Jacobian rule for components.

449
00:24:07,166 --> 00:24:09,629
The second kind of object is a covector.

450
00:24:09,629 --> 00:24:13,491
Picture it as a stack of parallel level lines, like the contour lines of a

451
00:24:13,491 --> 00:24:14,741
function on a map:

452
00:24:14,741 --> 00:24:16,529
the gradient of the function.

453
00:24:16,529 --> 00:24:19,329
A covector eats a vector and returns a number:

454
00:24:19,329 --> 00:24:21,704
how many of its lines the arrow crosses.

455
00:24:21,704 --> 00:24:23,229
Here, about four.

456
00:24:23,229 --> 00:24:27,641
In components, that's omega mu times V mu, summed, with the index down on

457
00:24:27,641 --> 00:24:29,533
omega and up on V.

458
00:24:29,533 --> 00:24:34,658
When coordinates change, a covector's components change with the inverse Jacobian,

459
00:24:34,658 --> 00:24:36,808
the opposite way to a vector's.

460
00:24:36,808 --> 00:24:40,033
That's what upper and lower indices keep track of.

461
00:24:40,033 --> 00:24:43,446
And it's exactly what's needed for the count to come out the same in every

462
00:24:43,446 --> 00:24:45,033
coordinate system:

463
00:24:45,033 --> 00:24:49,596
the number of lines an arrow crosses is a fact about the arrow and the lines,

464
00:24:49,596 --> 00:24:52,408
not about the labels.

465
00:24:52,558 --> 00:24:55,646
From vectors and covectors we build everything else.

466
00:24:55,646 --> 00:24:59,621
A tensor can have any number of upper and lower indices, and under a change

467
00:24:59,621 --> 00:25:03,308
of coordinates it picks up one Jacobian factor per index:

468
00:25:03,308 --> 00:25:07,808
the upper ones transform like vectors, the lower ones like covectors.

469
00:25:07,808 --> 00:25:09,783
Here's why this is worth the trouble:

470
00:25:09,783 --> 00:25:12,996
the transformation is linear and homogeneous.

471
00:25:12,996 --> 00:25:17,558
So if all of a tensor's components vanish in one coordinate system, they vanish

472
00:25:17,558 --> 00:25:19,908
in every coordinate system.

473
00:25:19,908 --> 00:25:23,883
A tensor equation, true for one observer, is true for all.

474
00:25:23,883 --> 00:25:25,896
That tells us what we're looking for:

475
00:25:25,896 --> 00:25:30,358
curvature must be measured by a tensor built from the metric, one that vanishes

476
00:25:30,358 --> 00:25:34,133
exactly when spacetime is flat.

477
00:25:34,266 --> 00:25:39,041
The most important tensor in this story is the metric, g mu nu, with two lower

478
00:25:39,041 --> 00:25:40,191
indices.

479
00:25:40,191 --> 00:25:44,366
It's a machine that takes two vectors and returns their dot product, and so

480
00:25:44,366 --> 00:25:47,754
the length of any vector, and the angle between any two.

481
00:25:47,754 --> 00:25:50,391
Everything geometric starts here.

482
00:25:50,391 --> 00:25:53,379
The metric also converts vectors into covectors.

483
00:25:53,379 --> 00:25:56,416
Feed it just one vector, and what's left is a covector:

484
00:25:56,416 --> 00:25:58,416
V with its index lowered.

485
00:25:58,416 --> 00:26:02,766
Geometrically, the arrow becomes the stack of lines perpendicular to it, spaced

486
00:26:02,766 --> 00:26:06,616
so that the arrow crosses exactly its own length squared of them.

487
00:26:06,616 --> 00:26:11,079
The inverse metric, g with upper indices, goes back the other way.

488
00:26:11,079 --> 00:26:15,366
From now on, raising and lowering indices with the metric is routine.

489
00:26:17,047 --> 00:26:20,609
Free particles in flat spacetime move on straight worldlines.

490
00:26:20,609 --> 00:26:23,697
In curved spacetime, what's the straightest possible path?

491
00:26:23,697 --> 00:26:28,059
On a sphere, take two cities on the sixtieth parallel, ninety degrees of longitude

492
00:26:28,059 --> 00:26:28,884
apart.

493
00:26:28,884 --> 00:26:33,159
Following the parallel takes zero point seven eight five times the radius.

494
00:26:33,159 --> 00:26:36,272
The great circle, which bulges toward the pole, is shorter:

495
00:26:36,272 --> 00:26:38,272
zero point seven two three.

496
00:26:38,272 --> 00:26:40,997
It's the shortest path, and it's also the straightest:

497
00:26:40,997 --> 00:26:44,297
an ant walking it never turns left or right.

498
00:26:44,297 --> 00:26:48,747
We'll define a geodesic as a path whose length, or in spacetime whose proper

499
00:26:48,747 --> 00:26:50,734
time, is stationary:

500
00:26:50,734 --> 00:26:55,772
it doesn't change, to first order, if you wiggle the path a little.

501
00:26:55,947 --> 00:26:58,947
Let's find the equation a geodesic satisfies.

502
00:26:58,947 --> 00:27:01,184
It's easiest to work with this integral:

503
00:27:01,184 --> 00:27:06,447
one half g mu nu times the velocity twice, along a path with parameter lambda.

504
00:27:06,447 --> 00:27:11,084
Its stationary paths are the geodesics, traversed at constant speed.

505
00:27:11,084 --> 00:27:16,034
The condition for a stationary path is the Euler–Lagrange equation, and differentiating

506
00:27:16,034 --> 00:27:20,572
the Lagrangian gives g sigma nu x dot nu for the first part, and half the

507
00:27:20,572 --> 00:27:23,238
derivative of the metric for the second.

508
00:27:23,238 --> 00:27:25,638
Now take the derivative along the path.

509
00:27:25,638 --> 00:27:30,051
The velocity changes, giving the acceleration term, and the metric changes

510
00:27:30,051 --> 00:27:33,651
too, because we're moving through a region where it varies.

511
00:27:33,651 --> 00:27:37,938
The middle term is summed over mu and nu against a symmetric product, so we

512
00:27:37,938 --> 00:27:41,738
can split it into two equal halves with mu and nu swapped.

513
00:27:41,738 --> 00:27:46,555
Finally, multiply by the inverse metric to free the acceleration.

514
00:27:46,555 --> 00:27:48,805
This is the geodesic equation.

515
00:27:48,805 --> 00:27:53,255
The acceleration, in coordinates, equals minus these coefficients times the

516
00:27:53,255 --> 00:27:54,868
velocity squared.

517
00:27:54,868 --> 00:27:58,968
The coefficients, built from first derivatives of the metric, are the Christoffel

518
00:27:58,968 --> 00:28:00,505
symbols, gamma.

519
00:28:00,505 --> 00:28:06,455
If the metric is constant, they vanish, and geodesics are straight lines.

520
00:28:06,613 --> 00:28:08,251
Let's try it on the sphere.

521
00:28:08,251 --> 00:28:11,863
Plugging its metric into the formula, only two kinds of Christoffel symbols

522
00:28:11,863 --> 00:28:12,826
survive.

523
00:28:12,826 --> 00:28:15,401
So geodesics obey these two equations.

524
00:28:15,401 --> 00:28:19,038
Start at one point, pick a direction, and integrate them numerically.

525
00:28:19,038 --> 00:28:22,663
Do it in twelve directions, and each solution is a great circle.

526
00:28:22,663 --> 00:28:23,613
And notice:

527
00:28:23,613 --> 00:28:26,801
they leave the starting point spreading apart, but they all meet again on

528
00:28:26,801 --> 00:28:28,101
the far side.

529
00:28:28,101 --> 00:28:31,788
Straight lines that start out diverging, then converge.

530
00:28:31,788 --> 00:28:32,951
Hold on to that:

531
00:28:32,951 --> 00:28:37,088
it's the curvature, showing up in the geodesics.

532
00:28:37,247 --> 00:28:39,234
One warning about these symbols.

533
00:28:39,234 --> 00:28:43,522
In polar coordinates on a flat plane, the Christoffel symbols aren't zero.

534
00:28:43,522 --> 00:28:47,359
Gamma measures how the coordinate grid itself bends and stretches, not whether

535
00:28:47,359 --> 00:28:48,897
the space is curved.

536
00:28:48,897 --> 00:28:54,234
Unlike a tensor, it can be nonzero in one coordinate system and zero in another.

537
00:28:54,234 --> 00:28:58,922
In fact, at any single point of any curved space, you can always choose coordinates

538
00:28:58,922 --> 00:29:03,047
in which the metric is the flat one and its first derivatives vanish, so every

539
00:29:03,047 --> 00:29:05,022
gamma is zero there.

540
00:29:05,022 --> 00:29:09,247
In spacetime, those are the coordinates of a freely falling observer.

541
00:29:09,247 --> 00:29:13,209
This is the equivalence principle, written as mathematics:

542
00:29:13,209 --> 00:29:17,647
at a point, gravity can be transformed away.

543
00:29:17,838 --> 00:29:22,363
Now apply the geodesic equation to a slowly moving particle in the weak, static

544
00:29:22,363 --> 00:29:24,276
field we found earlier.

545
00:29:24,276 --> 00:29:26,988
Look at the space components of the acceleration.

546
00:29:26,988 --> 00:29:31,713
For a slow particle, the time component of the velocity, about c, is enormous

547
00:29:31,713 --> 00:29:36,313
compared with the others, so only the gamma with two time indices matters.

548
00:29:36,313 --> 00:29:41,338
And with g zero zero equal to minus one plus two phi over c squared, that

549
00:29:41,338 --> 00:29:45,063
gamma is just the gradient of phi over c squared.

550
00:29:45,063 --> 00:29:45,863
The result:

551
00:29:45,863 --> 00:29:49,038
the acceleration is minus the gradient of phi.

552
00:29:49,038 --> 00:29:52,347
Newton's law, from the geodesic equation.

553
00:29:52,347 --> 00:29:56,547
So the Newtonian force of gravity lives in the Christoffel symbols.

554
00:29:56,547 --> 00:30:00,034
That explains why it can be transformed away by falling:

555
00:30:00,034 --> 00:30:04,759
gamma isn't a tensor, and in freely falling coordinates it vanishes.

556
00:30:04,759 --> 00:30:07,584
What can't be transformed away is curvature.

557
00:30:07,584 --> 00:30:11,472
To detect it, we need to compare directions at different points, and that

558
00:30:11,472 --> 00:30:13,172
requires one more tool.

559
00:30:14,819 --> 00:30:18,469
To detect curvature, we need to compare vectors at different points, and that

560
00:30:18,469 --> 00:30:20,832
turns out to be surprisingly subtle.

561
00:30:20,832 --> 00:30:23,244
Here's the simplest possible vector field:

562
00:30:23,244 --> 00:30:26,894
the same arrow, pointing right, everywhere on a flat plane.

563
00:30:26,894 --> 00:30:28,744
Its rate of change is zero.

564
00:30:28,744 --> 00:30:31,244
But describe it in polar coordinates.

565
00:30:31,244 --> 00:30:35,294
At each point, the basis vectors point outward and around, and they rotate

566
00:30:35,294 --> 00:30:36,707
as you go around.

567
00:30:36,707 --> 00:30:42,619
So the components of our constant arrow, V r and V phi, keep changing.

568
00:30:43,261 --> 00:30:47,136
Take the ordinary partial derivative of a component, and you get something

569
00:30:47,136 --> 00:30:50,648
nonzero, for a field that isn't changing at all.

570
00:30:50,648 --> 00:30:53,948
The partial derivative of components mixes up two things:

571
00:30:53,948 --> 00:30:57,323
how the vector changes, and how the basis changes.

572
00:30:57,323 --> 00:31:00,536
Only the first is physical.

573
00:31:00,686 --> 00:31:02,173
So let's separate them.

574
00:31:02,173 --> 00:31:05,261
Write the vector as components times basis vectors.

575
00:31:05,261 --> 00:31:08,211
Its derivative has two parts, by the product rule:

576
00:31:08,211 --> 00:31:11,998
the change in the components, and the change in the basis vectors.

577
00:31:11,998 --> 00:31:15,948
The change of a basis vector is itself a vector, so it's some combination

578
00:31:15,948 --> 00:31:20,723
of the basis vectors, and the coefficients are exactly the Christoffel symbols.

579
00:31:20,723 --> 00:31:22,523
That's what gamma really is:

580
00:31:22,523 --> 00:31:26,286
how the basis turns and stretches from point to point.

581
00:31:26,286 --> 00:31:29,511
Collect the components, and you get the covariant derivative:

582
00:31:29,511 --> 00:31:33,823
the partial derivative, plus a gamma term that corrects for the turning basis.

583
00:31:33,823 --> 00:31:36,586
Unlike the partial derivative, it's a tensor.

584
00:31:36,586 --> 00:31:38,361
Check it on our constant field:

585
00:31:38,361 --> 00:31:42,586
the derivative of V r around the circle was minus sine phi, and the gamma

586
00:31:42,586 --> 00:31:44,523
term is plus sine phi.

587
00:31:44,523 --> 00:31:45,723
They cancel.

588
00:31:45,723 --> 00:31:51,386
The covariant derivative of a constant field is zero, as it should be.

589
00:31:51,519 --> 00:31:56,369
Covectors get a minus gamma term instead, and a general tensor gets one correction

590
00:31:56,369 --> 00:31:57,669
per index.

591
00:31:57,669 --> 00:32:00,819
Now, which gamma is the right one on a curved space?

592
00:32:00,819 --> 00:32:02,894
We ask for two natural things.

593
00:32:02,894 --> 00:32:06,794
First, carrying vectors around shouldn't change their lengths or the angles

594
00:32:06,794 --> 00:32:07,744
between them:

595
00:32:07,744 --> 00:32:10,657
the covariant derivative of the metric is zero.

596
00:32:10,657 --> 00:32:12,357
Second, no twisting:

597
00:32:12,357 --> 00:32:15,619
gamma is symmetric in its lower indices.

598
00:32:15,619 --> 00:32:19,982
Write out the first condition three times with the indices permuted, add two

599
00:32:19,982 --> 00:32:24,307
and subtract the third, and gamma is forced to be exactly the Christoffel

600
00:32:24,307 --> 00:32:27,007
formula we found from geodesics.

601
00:32:27,007 --> 00:32:33,194
Straightness and parallelism come from the same object, built from the metric.

602
00:32:33,327 --> 00:32:36,852
With the covariant derivative we can finally say what it means to carry a

603
00:32:36,852 --> 00:32:39,490
vector along a path without turning it.

604
00:32:39,490 --> 00:32:43,615
Along a curve, the vector's covariant rate of change is zero:

605
00:32:43,615 --> 00:32:48,202
its components change only as much as needed to keep up with the turning basis.

606
00:32:48,202 --> 00:32:53,402
This is parallel transport, the ant's rule from the triangle, as an equation.

607
00:32:53,402 --> 00:32:57,140
And if the vector you carry is the curve's own velocity, you get back the

608
00:32:57,140 --> 00:32:59,127
geodesic equation.

609
00:32:59,127 --> 00:33:03,240
A geodesic is a curve that parallel transports its own direction:

610
00:33:03,240 --> 00:33:05,902
it never turns.

611
00:33:06,077 --> 00:33:08,865
Let's watch parallel transport on the sphere.

612
00:33:08,865 --> 00:33:12,715
Carry an arrow once around the circle sixty degrees down from the north pole,

613
00:33:12,715 --> 00:33:14,515
following the rule exactly:

614
00:33:14,515 --> 00:33:18,065
here, the transport equation is integrated numerically.

615
00:33:18,065 --> 00:33:22,577
Halfway around, the arrow has already swung far from where it started, relative

616
00:33:22,577 --> 00:33:24,752
to the directions north and east.

617
00:33:24,752 --> 00:33:27,765
And when it gets back, it's pointing the opposite way:

618
00:33:27,765 --> 00:33:31,852
rotated by a hundred and eighty degrees.

619
00:33:32,044 --> 00:33:36,132
In general, around the circle at angle theta zero from the pole, the arrow

620
00:33:36,132 --> 00:33:40,769
turns by two pi times one minus cosine theta zero, which is the area of the

621
00:33:40,769 --> 00:33:44,469
polar cap inside the loop, divided by R squared.

622
00:33:44,469 --> 00:33:45,594
Sixty degrees:

623
00:33:45,594 --> 00:33:46,794
a half turn.

624
00:33:46,794 --> 00:33:47,832
Thirty degrees:

625
00:33:47,832 --> 00:33:49,344
forty-eight degrees.

626
00:33:49,344 --> 00:33:55,519
And around the equator, which is itself a geodesic, no rotation at all.

627
00:33:55,652 --> 00:33:58,190
You can see this experiment in a museum.

628
00:33:58,190 --> 00:34:03,090
A Foucault pendulum, first hung in Paris in 1851, swings back and forth in

629
00:34:03,090 --> 00:34:06,440
a plane that the pendulum keeps as parallel as it can.

630
00:34:06,440 --> 00:34:10,352
The Earth's rotation carries it around its circle of latitude once a day,

631
00:34:10,352 --> 00:34:14,402
so the swing plane is parallel transported around that circle, and relative

632
00:34:14,402 --> 00:34:18,090
to the floor it turns by three hundred and sixty degrees times the sine of

633
00:34:18,090 --> 00:34:19,290
the latitude:

634
00:34:19,290 --> 00:34:22,227
about two hundred and seventy degrees a day in Paris.

635
00:34:23,883 --> 00:34:25,233
Back to curvature.

636
00:34:25,233 --> 00:34:28,871
We saw that an arrow carried around a loop comes back rotated.

637
00:34:28,871 --> 00:34:32,796
Here's the rotation for twelve loops of different sizes on a unit sphere,

638
00:34:32,796 --> 00:34:35,371
plotted against the area they enclose.

639
00:34:35,371 --> 00:34:37,583
A perfect straight line of slope one:

640
00:34:37,583 --> 00:34:41,871
the rotation is proportional to the area, and the constant of proportionality

641
00:34:41,871 --> 00:34:44,871
is the curvature, one over R squared.

642
00:34:44,871 --> 00:34:48,141
Curvature is rotation per unit area.

643
00:34:48,141 --> 00:34:52,241
In more dimensions, a loop can lie in many different planes, and the arrow

644
00:34:52,241 --> 00:34:54,679
can be rotated in many different ways.

645
00:34:54,679 --> 00:34:59,054
So for a tiny loop, built by going along delta a and then delta b, the change

646
00:34:59,054 --> 00:35:02,391
in the vector is a linear function of the vector and of both sides of the

647
00:35:02,391 --> 00:35:03,079
loop:

648
00:35:03,079 --> 00:35:05,279
a tensor with four indices.

649
00:35:05,279 --> 00:35:09,079
One says which component of the change we're looking at, one which component

650
00:35:09,079 --> 00:35:12,791
of the vector caused it, and two describe the plane of the loop.

651
00:35:12,791 --> 00:35:16,591
This is the Riemann curvature tensor.

652
00:35:16,775 --> 00:35:18,025
Let's compute it.

653
00:35:18,025 --> 00:35:22,500
Going around a tiny loop, first one way and then the other, amounts to taking

654
00:35:22,500 --> 00:35:25,462
covariant derivatives in two different orders.

655
00:35:25,462 --> 00:35:29,087
So the curvature is the commutator of covariant derivatives:

656
00:35:29,087 --> 00:35:31,587
how much the answer depends on the order.

657
00:35:31,587 --> 00:35:35,400
Expand both covariant derivatives using their definition.

658
00:35:35,400 --> 00:35:39,158
It's a page of terms, but it collapses beautifully.

659
00:35:39,158 --> 00:35:43,621
The second derivatives of V cancel, because partial derivatives commute.

660
00:35:43,621 --> 00:35:47,296
The terms with one derivative of V cancel in pairs.

661
00:35:47,296 --> 00:35:49,996
What's left has no derivatives of V at all:

662
00:35:49,996 --> 00:35:55,916
just V, multiplied by a combination of the Christoffel symbols and their derivatives.

663
00:35:55,916 --> 00:35:58,579
That combination is the Riemann tensor:

664
00:35:58,579 --> 00:36:02,666
two derivatives of the Christoffel symbols, plus two products of them.

665
00:36:02,666 --> 00:36:06,704
And because the derivatives of V dropped out, the commutator is just R times

666
00:36:06,704 --> 00:36:08,804
V, at a single point.

667
00:36:08,804 --> 00:36:14,266
That's why R is a genuine tensor, even though gamma isn't.

668
00:36:14,466 --> 00:36:17,829
Riemann is the coordinate-independent test we were looking for.

669
00:36:17,829 --> 00:36:21,216
A space is flat exactly when it vanishes everywhere.

670
00:36:21,216 --> 00:36:25,629
The polar plane has nonzero gammas but zero Riemann tensor.

671
00:36:25,629 --> 00:36:26,941
The sphere doesn't:

672
00:36:26,941 --> 00:36:31,341
computing from its metric gives R theta phi theta phi equal to sine squared

673
00:36:31,341 --> 00:36:32,191
theta.

674
00:36:32,191 --> 00:36:33,454
It has symmetries:

675
00:36:33,454 --> 00:36:37,916
antisymmetric in each pair of indices, symmetric under swapping the pairs,

676
00:36:37,916 --> 00:36:39,991
and a cyclic identity.

677
00:36:39,991 --> 00:36:44,791
So in n dimensions it has n squared times n squared minus one over twelve

678
00:36:44,791 --> 00:36:46,591
independent components:

679
00:36:46,591 --> 00:36:51,554
one for a surface, which is Gauss's curvature, six in three dimensions, and

680
00:36:51,554 --> 00:36:54,483
twenty in four-dimensional spacetime.

681
00:36:54,483 --> 00:36:56,971
Two averages of it will matter most.

682
00:36:56,971 --> 00:37:01,758
Contract the first and third indices, and you get the Ricci tensor, R mu nu,

683
00:37:01,758 --> 00:37:03,371
with ten components.

684
00:37:03,371 --> 00:37:07,121
Contract again with the inverse metric, and you get a single number at each

685
00:37:07,121 --> 00:37:09,071
point, the Ricci scalar.

686
00:37:09,071 --> 00:37:12,883
For a sphere it's two over R squared.

687
00:37:13,016 --> 00:37:14,929
And now the payoff for physics.

688
00:37:14,929 --> 00:37:18,616
Start two geodesics on the equator, both heading north, parallel.

689
00:37:18,616 --> 00:37:20,541
On a sphere they don't stay parallel:

690
00:37:20,541 --> 00:37:23,366
their separation shrinks, and they meet at the pole.

691
00:37:23,366 --> 00:37:28,004
The separation, xi, accelerates toward zero at a rate set by the curvature.

692
00:37:28,004 --> 00:37:32,104
In general, the relative acceleration of two neighboring geodesics is the

693
00:37:32,104 --> 00:37:37,516
Riemann tensor, contracted twice with the velocity, and once with their separation.

694
00:37:37,516 --> 00:37:41,691
This is the geodesic deviation equation.

695
00:37:41,900 --> 00:37:46,087
Now apply it to slowly moving particles in a weak gravitational field.

696
00:37:46,087 --> 00:37:50,062
Their separation accelerates according to minus c squared times the components

697
00:37:50,062 --> 00:37:52,450
of Riemann with two time indices.

698
00:37:52,450 --> 00:37:56,362
Newton's theory has its own version, the tidal acceleration:

699
00:37:56,362 --> 00:38:00,137
minus the second derivatives of the potential times the separation.

700
00:38:00,137 --> 00:38:02,400
These must agree, and they do:

701
00:38:02,400 --> 00:38:07,225
for the weak-field metric, R i zero j zero is exactly the second derivative

702
00:38:07,225 --> 00:38:09,500
of phi over c squared.

703
00:38:09,500 --> 00:38:13,550
The tidal tensor of Newton is the curvature of spacetime.

704
00:38:13,550 --> 00:38:16,025
Our falling ring is a curvature detector.

705
00:38:16,025 --> 00:38:19,625
And its most important reading is the trace, the sum of the diagonal tidal

706
00:38:19,625 --> 00:38:22,825
terms, which is the Ricci component R zero zero:

707
00:38:22,825 --> 00:38:24,537
the Laplacian of phi.

708
00:38:24,537 --> 00:38:27,700
In empty space around the Earth, the Laplacian is zero:

709
00:38:27,700 --> 00:38:31,450
the stretching along the fall exactly balances the squeezing in the two sideways

710
00:38:31,450 --> 00:38:35,212
directions, so a ball of particles keeps its volume at first.

711
00:38:35,212 --> 00:38:39,125
Inside matter, it's four pi G rho, and the ring shrinks.

712
00:38:39,125 --> 00:38:41,975
Matter shows up in the trace of the curvature.

713
00:38:41,975 --> 00:38:43,625
That's the clue Einstein needed.

714
00:38:48,930 --> 00:38:50,455
Now the other side of the equation:

715
00:38:50,455 --> 00:38:51,293
matter.

716
00:38:51,293 --> 00:38:54,955
In Newton's theory, the source of gravity is the mass density, rho.

717
00:38:54,955 --> 00:38:59,118
But relativity says mass is a form of energy, E equals m c squared, so the

718
00:38:59,118 --> 00:39:01,005
source should be energy density.

719
00:39:01,005 --> 00:39:02,880
Here's a box of dust at rest.

720
00:39:02,880 --> 00:39:05,393
Its energy density is rho c squared.

721
00:39:05,393 --> 00:39:08,555
Now look at the same dust from a frame where it's moving at six tenths the

722
00:39:08,555 --> 00:39:09,668
speed of light.

723
00:39:09,668 --> 00:39:11,105
Two things change.

724
00:39:11,105 --> 00:39:14,968
The box is length-contracted, so the particles are packed more densely:

725
00:39:14,968 --> 00:39:16,855
one factor of gamma.

726
00:39:16,855 --> 00:39:20,193
And each particle is moving, so it carries more energy:

727
00:39:20,193 --> 00:39:21,930
a second factor of gamma.

728
00:39:21,930 --> 00:39:25,280
The energy density goes up by gamma squared.

729
00:39:25,280 --> 00:39:27,268
A scalar wouldn't change at all.

730
00:39:27,268 --> 00:39:30,930
A vector's time component would pick up one factor of gamma.

731
00:39:30,930 --> 00:39:35,380
Two factors of gamma is the signature of a tensor with two indices.

732
00:39:35,380 --> 00:39:38,480
So the source of gravity can't be a single number.

733
00:39:38,480 --> 00:39:43,605
It has to be the time-time component of a two-index tensor.

734
00:39:43,747 --> 00:39:47,097
That tensor is the stress–energy tensor, T mu nu.

735
00:39:47,097 --> 00:39:47,984
Its meaning:

736
00:39:47,984 --> 00:39:51,434
T mu nu is the flux of the mu component of four-momentum through a surface

737
00:39:51,434 --> 00:39:52,984
of constant x nu.

738
00:39:52,984 --> 00:39:54,709
Let's read it block by block.

739
00:39:54,709 --> 00:39:58,847
T zero zero, momentum's time component, energy, crossing a surface of constant

740
00:39:58,847 --> 00:39:59,447
time:

741
00:39:59,447 --> 00:40:01,572
that's simply the energy density.

742
00:40:01,572 --> 00:40:03,847
The rest of the top row and the first column:

743
00:40:03,847 --> 00:40:07,872
the flow of energy, which is the same thing as the density of momentum.

744
00:40:07,872 --> 00:40:10,672
And the space-space block is the flow of momentum:

745
00:40:10,672 --> 00:40:11,972
stress.

746
00:40:11,972 --> 00:40:16,197
On the diagonal, momentum in x flowing across a surface facing x:

747
00:40:16,197 --> 00:40:17,459
that's pressure.

748
00:40:17,459 --> 00:40:18,672
Off the diagonal:

749
00:40:18,672 --> 00:40:19,622
shear.

750
00:40:19,622 --> 00:40:23,347
The tensor is symmetric, so it has ten independent components:

751
00:40:23,347 --> 00:40:26,722
the same number as the metric.

752
00:40:26,880 --> 00:40:30,168
For most of what follows, matter will be a perfect fluid:

753
00:40:30,168 --> 00:40:34,005
something with a density rho and a pressure p, and no viscosity.

754
00:40:34,005 --> 00:40:39,143
Its stress–energy tensor has this form, where u is the fluid's four-velocity.

755
00:40:39,143 --> 00:40:41,643
In the fluid's own rest frame it's diagonal:

756
00:40:41,643 --> 00:40:45,880
energy density in the corner, and the pressure in all three space directions.

757
00:40:45,880 --> 00:40:48,155
And with no pressure at all, it's dust:

758
00:40:48,155 --> 00:40:53,355
just rho times u times u, the boosted box we just saw.

759
00:40:53,538 --> 00:40:56,026
One more property, and it's the crucial one.

760
00:40:56,026 --> 00:41:00,363
Energy and momentum are conserved, and for a tensor of fluxes that has a compact

761
00:41:00,363 --> 00:41:01,063
form:

762
00:41:01,063 --> 00:41:03,126
the divergence of T is zero.

763
00:41:03,126 --> 00:41:06,863
Whatever flows out of a small region is lost from inside it.

764
00:41:06,863 --> 00:41:11,063
The time component of this equation is the continuity equation, conservation

765
00:41:11,063 --> 00:41:12,901
of mass and energy.

766
00:41:12,901 --> 00:41:15,876
The space components are conservation of momentum:

767
00:41:15,876 --> 00:41:18,326
Euler's equation of fluid motion.

768
00:41:18,326 --> 00:41:22,205
So all of fluid dynamics is inside this one line.

769
00:41:22,205 --> 00:41:26,655
In curved spacetime, partial derivatives become covariant derivatives:

770
00:41:26,655 --> 00:41:30,380
the divergence of T, taken with nabla, vanishes.

771
00:41:30,380 --> 00:41:32,718
Now here's why this matters so much.

772
00:41:32,718 --> 00:41:36,430
Whatever we put on the geometry side of Einstein's equation, it will equal

773
00:41:36,430 --> 00:41:37,968
a constant times T.

774
00:41:37,968 --> 00:41:42,593
So it must have zero divergence too, and not just for special solutions:

775
00:41:42,593 --> 00:41:46,730
automatically, as an identity, whatever the matter happens to be doing.

776
00:41:48,378 --> 00:41:50,703
We're ready to find Einstein's equation.

777
00:41:50,703 --> 00:41:52,866
Like Poisson's equation, it should say:

778
00:41:52,866 --> 00:41:56,103
something geometric equals a constant times the matter.

779
00:41:56,103 --> 00:41:59,041
The matter side is the stress–energy tensor.

780
00:41:59,041 --> 00:42:00,353
What goes on the left?

781
00:42:00,353 --> 00:42:02,703
It has to satisfy four requirements.

782
00:42:02,703 --> 00:42:06,366
It has to be a symmetric tensor with two indices, to match T.

783
00:42:06,366 --> 00:42:09,991
It should be built from the metric and its first and second derivatives, just

784
00:42:09,991 --> 00:42:13,028
as Newton's equation has two derivatives of phi.

785
00:42:13,028 --> 00:42:17,053
It has to be divergence-free automatically, because T is.

786
00:42:17,053 --> 00:42:22,953
And in the limit of weak fields and slow motion, it has to give back Newton.

787
00:42:23,078 --> 00:42:25,616
Start from the clue at the end of the last chapter.

788
00:42:25,616 --> 00:42:28,828
Newton says the Laplacian of phi is four pi G rho.

789
00:42:28,828 --> 00:42:32,878
And the Laplacian of phi is the trace of the tidal tensor, which is c squared

790
00:42:32,878 --> 00:42:35,353
times the Ricci component R zero zero.

791
00:42:35,353 --> 00:42:37,916
And rho c squared is T zero zero.

792
00:42:37,916 --> 00:42:41,716
So Newton's law of gravity is one component of an equation between Ricci and

793
00:42:41,716 --> 00:42:42,366
T.

794
00:42:42,366 --> 00:42:45,716
The natural guess is that it holds for every component:

795
00:42:45,716 --> 00:42:47,628
Ricci equals kappa times T.

796
00:42:47,628 --> 00:42:53,203
Einstein proposed essentially this in early November 1915.

797
00:42:53,320 --> 00:42:56,295
But first we need one more fact about curvature.

798
00:42:56,295 --> 00:42:58,995
The Riemann tensor obeys a differential identity:

799
00:42:58,995 --> 00:43:03,720
the cyclic sum of its covariant derivatives over the first three indices vanishes.

800
00:43:03,720 --> 00:43:05,845
This is the Bianchi identity.

801
00:43:05,845 --> 00:43:07,282
The proof is short:

802
00:43:07,282 --> 00:43:11,420
go to a freely falling frame at a point, where every gamma vanishes.

803
00:43:11,420 --> 00:43:15,795
There, Riemann is just derivatives of gamma, and the cyclic sum cancels term

804
00:43:15,795 --> 00:43:16,820
by term.

805
00:43:16,820 --> 00:43:21,503
And since it's a tensor equation, it holds in every coordinate system.

806
00:43:21,503 --> 00:43:25,341
Now contract it with the metric, pairing the derivative's index with Riemann's

807
00:43:25,341 --> 00:43:26,678
third index.

808
00:43:26,678 --> 00:43:29,691
Two of the Riemann tensors become Ricci tensors.

809
00:43:29,691 --> 00:43:33,928
Contract once more, and everything is Ricci or the Ricci scalar:

810
00:43:33,928 --> 00:43:36,891
the divergence of Ricci equals half the gradient of R.

811
00:43:36,891 --> 00:43:39,553
Move everything inside one derivative.

812
00:43:39,553 --> 00:43:45,028
The combination R mu nu minus one half R g mu nu has zero divergence, always,

813
00:43:45,028 --> 00:43:48,328
for any metric whatsoever.

814
00:43:48,528 --> 00:43:49,941
Now test the guess.

815
00:43:49,941 --> 00:43:52,291
Take the divergence of both sides.

816
00:43:52,291 --> 00:43:56,953
The right side vanishes, because matter conserves energy and momentum.

817
00:43:56,953 --> 00:44:01,128
But by the Bianchi identity, the divergence of Ricci is half the gradient

818
00:44:01,128 --> 00:44:02,541
of the Ricci scalar.

819
00:44:02,541 --> 00:44:05,578
So the Ricci scalar would have to be constant.

820
00:44:05,578 --> 00:44:09,603
And taking the trace of the guess, R is kappa times the trace of T.

821
00:44:09,603 --> 00:44:12,641
So the trace of T would have to be constant too:

822
00:44:12,641 --> 00:44:17,278
for ordinary matter that's minus the energy density plus three times the pressure,

823
00:44:17,278 --> 00:44:22,391
the same value everywhere in the universe, inside stars and in empty space.

824
00:44:22,391 --> 00:44:23,641
That's absurd.

825
00:44:23,641 --> 00:44:26,478
The guess is wrong.

826
00:44:26,587 --> 00:44:29,812
But the Bianchi identity also tells us how to fix it.

827
00:44:29,812 --> 00:44:34,087
The combination R mu nu minus one half R g mu nu is divergence-free for every

828
00:44:34,087 --> 00:44:35,012
metric.

829
00:44:35,012 --> 00:44:37,749
It's called the Einstein tensor, G mu nu.

830
00:44:37,749 --> 00:44:39,512
So set it equal to kappa times T.

831
00:44:39,512 --> 00:44:43,574
Now the divergence of the left side is zero identically, and conservation

832
00:44:43,574 --> 00:44:47,162
of energy and momentum is built in, not imposed.

833
00:44:47,162 --> 00:44:48,974
One more term is allowed:

834
00:44:48,974 --> 00:44:54,487
a constant times the metric itself, since the metric has zero covariant derivative.

835
00:44:54,487 --> 00:44:59,612
That's the cosmological constant, lambda, which Einstein added in 1917.

836
00:44:59,612 --> 00:45:01,387
Three requirements down.

837
00:45:01,387 --> 00:45:05,312
The fourth, Newton, will fix kappa.

838
00:45:05,478 --> 00:45:08,853
Start from G equals kappa T, and take the trace.

839
00:45:08,853 --> 00:45:13,566
The trace of the metric is four, so the left side becomes R minus two R:

840
00:45:13,566 --> 00:45:16,153
minus R equals kappa times the trace of T.

841
00:45:16,153 --> 00:45:20,228
Substitute back, and we get an equivalent form, with the trace moved to the

842
00:45:20,228 --> 00:45:21,566
matter side:

843
00:45:21,566 --> 00:45:25,762
Ricci equals kappa times T minus one half T g.

844
00:45:25,762 --> 00:45:27,712
Now the Newtonian situation:

845
00:45:27,712 --> 00:45:32,799
slowly moving dust, so T zero zero is rho c squared and the trace is minus

846
00:45:32,799 --> 00:45:34,324
rho c squared.

847
00:45:34,324 --> 00:45:39,262
The time-time component gives R zero zero equals one half kappa rho c squared.

848
00:45:39,262 --> 00:45:43,324
But R zero zero is the Laplacian of phi over c squared.

849
00:45:43,324 --> 00:45:48,049
For this to be Newton's four pi G rho, kappa must be eight pi G over c to

850
00:45:48,049 --> 00:45:50,287
the fourth.

851
00:45:50,445 --> 00:45:51,507
And there it is:

852
00:45:51,507 --> 00:45:53,470
Einstein's field equations.

853
00:45:53,470 --> 00:45:56,595
He presented them to the Prussian Academy on the twenty-fifth of November,

854
00:45:56,595 --> 00:46:00,220
1915, in the trace-reversed form we just derived;

855
00:46:00,220 --> 00:46:03,232
the cosmological constant came two years later.

856
00:46:03,232 --> 00:46:07,195
Curvature on the left, energy and momentum on the right, ten equations in

857
00:46:07,195 --> 00:46:07,907
all.

858
00:46:07,907 --> 00:46:10,020
Notice how small the constant is:

859
00:46:10,020 --> 00:46:13,857
about two times ten to the minus forty-three in SI units.

860
00:46:13,857 --> 00:46:16,870
Spacetime is extraordinarily stiff.

861
00:46:16,870 --> 00:46:21,920
It takes the energy density of a planet or a star to bend it noticeably.

862
00:46:21,920 --> 00:46:24,745
There's a bonus inside the trace-reversed form.

863
00:46:24,745 --> 00:46:28,932
For a fluid with pressure, the same calculation gives the Laplacian of phi

864
00:46:28,932 --> 00:46:33,382
equals four pi G times rho plus three p over c squared.

865
00:46:33,382 --> 00:46:36,432
In Einstein's theory, pressure gravitates too.

866
00:46:36,432 --> 00:46:39,295
That will matter for stars, and for the universe.

867
00:46:40,941 --> 00:46:43,529
What does Einstein's equation actually say?

868
00:46:43,529 --> 00:46:47,466
Ten coupled nonlinear equations are hard to picture, but there's a beautiful

869
00:46:47,466 --> 00:46:51,804
way to say it in one sentence, due to John Baez and Emory Bunn.

870
00:46:51,804 --> 00:46:56,191
Take a small ball of freely falling test particles, initially at rest relative

871
00:46:56,191 --> 00:46:57,379
to each other.

872
00:46:57,379 --> 00:47:01,179
The rate at which it begins to shrink is proportional to its volume, times

873
00:47:01,179 --> 00:47:05,516
the energy density at its center, plus the pressure in the x direction, plus

874
00:47:05,516 --> 00:47:09,837
the pressure in the y direction, plus the pressure in the z direction.

875
00:47:09,837 --> 00:47:11,987
We've already done the work to see why.

876
00:47:11,987 --> 00:47:16,087
The ball's volume responds to the trace of the tidal tensor, which is c squared

877
00:47:16,087 --> 00:47:17,487
R zero zero.

878
00:47:17,487 --> 00:47:21,462
And the trace-reversed field equation says that, for a fluid, c squared R

879
00:47:21,462 --> 00:47:26,025
zero zero is four pi G times rho plus three p over c squared.

880
00:47:26,025 --> 00:47:30,962
Remarkably, Baez and Bunn show that this one sentence, applied to every small

881
00:47:30,962 --> 00:47:35,237
ball in every freely falling frame, is equivalent to the whole of Einstein's

882
00:47:35,237 --> 00:47:37,587
equation.

883
00:47:37,787 --> 00:47:38,812
Let's watch it.

884
00:47:38,812 --> 00:47:42,562
On the left, a ball of test particles in empty space, falling toward a planet

885
00:47:42,562 --> 00:47:43,512
below.

886
00:47:43,512 --> 00:47:46,462
On the right, a ball inside a uniform cloud of dust.

887
00:47:46,462 --> 00:47:47,625
Release both.

888
00:47:47,625 --> 00:47:51,287
In empty space, the energy density is zero, so the volume doesn't change at

889
00:47:51,287 --> 00:47:52,212
first.

890
00:47:52,212 --> 00:47:53,875
The ball does change shape:

891
00:47:53,875 --> 00:47:57,575
tides stretch it toward the planet and squeeze it sideways, into an egg of

892
00:47:57,575 --> 00:48:00,112
almost exactly the same volume.

893
00:48:00,112 --> 00:48:05,812
Inside matter, the ball shrinks, at exactly the rate four pi G rho.

894
00:48:06,504 --> 00:48:08,841
This splits curvature into two kinds.

895
00:48:08,841 --> 00:48:12,491
Riemann's twenty components divide into ten that make up the Ricci tensor

896
00:48:12,491 --> 00:48:14,804
and ten more called the Weyl tensor.

897
00:48:14,804 --> 00:48:18,216
Weyl curvature changes the shape of the ball but not its volume:

898
00:48:18,216 --> 00:48:22,004
that's tides, and as we'll see, gravitational waves.

899
00:48:22,004 --> 00:48:26,304
Ricci curvature changes the volume, and Einstein's equation ties it directly

900
00:48:26,304 --> 00:48:28,041
to the matter at that point.

901
00:48:28,041 --> 00:48:30,504
The matter only fixes half of the curvature.

902
00:48:30,504 --> 00:48:33,479
The other half can travel through empty space.

903
00:48:33,479 --> 00:48:38,216
And of the ten equations, the Bianchi identity makes four automatic, leaving

904
00:48:38,216 --> 00:48:41,254
six that drive the geometry forward in time;

905
00:48:41,254 --> 00:48:46,929
the remaining four functions just reflect our freedom to choose coordinates.

906
00:48:47,125 --> 00:48:50,662
Finally, apply the same sentence to the biggest ball there is.

907
00:48:50,662 --> 00:48:54,612
In a universe filled uniformly with matter, any region is a ball of freely

908
00:48:54,612 --> 00:48:55,975
falling particles:

909
00:48:55,975 --> 00:48:57,225
the galaxies.

910
00:48:57,225 --> 00:49:01,650
Its radius obeys this equation, Friedmann's acceleration equation.

911
00:49:01,650 --> 00:49:04,650
Matter and pressure make the expansion slow down.

912
00:49:04,650 --> 00:49:08,650
But the cosmological constant behaves like an energy density with negative

913
00:49:08,650 --> 00:49:13,662
pressure, minus rho c squared, and then rho plus three p is negative:

914
00:49:13,662 --> 00:49:16,254
it makes the expansion speed up.

915
00:49:16,254 --> 00:49:20,391
Here's the size of such a ball over cosmic history, for the measured contents

916
00:49:20,391 --> 00:49:21,716
of our universe.

917
00:49:21,716 --> 00:49:26,079
For its first seven or eight billion years, the matter won, and the expansion

918
00:49:26,079 --> 00:49:27,466
decelerated.

919
00:49:27,466 --> 00:49:31,941
About seven point seven billion years after the Big Bang, the matter had thinned

920
00:49:31,941 --> 00:49:36,329
out enough for the cosmological constant to take over, and the expansion began

921
00:49:36,329 --> 00:49:40,379
to accelerate, as astronomers discovered in 1998.

922
00:49:41,848 --> 00:49:44,073
There's a second road to the same equation.

923
00:49:44,073 --> 00:49:48,635
David Hilbert took it, in a paper he submitted five days before Einstein presented

924
00:49:48,635 --> 00:49:50,048
his final version.

925
00:49:50,048 --> 00:49:54,248
We saw that a free particle moves so as to make its proper time, its action,

926
00:49:54,248 --> 00:49:55,485
stationary.

927
00:49:55,485 --> 00:49:59,985
A field's action is an integral over all of spacetime of a scalar Lagrangian,

928
00:49:59,985 --> 00:50:03,635
weighted by the square root of minus the determinant of the metric.

929
00:50:03,635 --> 00:50:06,473
So which scalar should the gravitational field use?

930
00:50:06,473 --> 00:50:10,123
It has to be built from the metric with at most two derivatives.

931
00:50:10,123 --> 00:50:13,023
Apart from a constant, there's essentially one choice:

932
00:50:13,023 --> 00:50:14,410
the Ricci scalar.

933
00:50:14,410 --> 00:50:17,185
This is the Einstein–Hilbert action.

934
00:50:17,185 --> 00:50:22,448
The constant in front is chosen so that Newton comes out right.

935
00:50:22,631 --> 00:50:24,393
First, that square root.

936
00:50:24,393 --> 00:50:28,506
A coordinate cell is a little parallelogram spanned by the basis vectors,

937
00:50:28,506 --> 00:50:32,193
and its true area is the square root of the determinant of the metric, times

938
00:50:32,193 --> 00:50:33,906
the coordinate steps.

939
00:50:33,906 --> 00:50:36,593
Change the metric, and the area changes.

940
00:50:36,593 --> 00:50:40,781
So the square root of minus g times d four x is the true four-dimensional

941
00:50:40,781 --> 00:50:44,056
volume, the same in every coordinate system.

942
00:50:44,056 --> 00:50:47,656
We'll need how it responds to a small change in the metric.

943
00:50:47,656 --> 00:50:51,281
Jacobi's formula for the derivative of a determinant gives it:

944
00:50:51,281 --> 00:50:56,768
the variation of root minus g is minus one half root minus g, g mu nu, delta

945
00:50:56,768 --> 00:50:59,431
g upper mu nu.

946
00:50:59,656 --> 00:51:01,268
Now vary the metric.

947
00:51:01,268 --> 00:51:02,606
Two things change:

948
00:51:02,606 --> 00:51:06,656
the volume factor, and the Ricci scalar, which is the inverse metric times

949
00:51:06,656 --> 00:51:07,618
Ricci.

950
00:51:07,618 --> 00:51:11,231
The volume factor gives minus one half R g mu nu.

951
00:51:11,231 --> 00:51:14,531
Varying the inverse metric in R gives R mu nu.

952
00:51:14,531 --> 00:51:18,806
And there's a leftover piece, the variation of Ricci itself.

953
00:51:18,806 --> 00:51:20,768
That leftover is harmless.

954
00:51:20,768 --> 00:51:24,681
The variation of the Riemann tensor is a difference of covariant derivatives

955
00:51:24,681 --> 00:51:29,131
of the variation of gamma, the Palatini identity, so the leftover term is

956
00:51:29,131 --> 00:51:30,806
a total divergence.

957
00:51:30,806 --> 00:51:34,581
It integrates to the boundary of the region, where we hold the metric fixed,

958
00:51:34,581 --> 00:51:35,893
and drops out.

959
00:51:35,893 --> 00:51:40,331
What survives is the Einstein tensor, times the variation of the metric.

960
00:51:40,331 --> 00:51:42,093
It appears here by itself:

961
00:51:42,093 --> 00:51:44,514
nobody had to guess the one half.

962
00:51:44,514 --> 00:51:48,364
For the matter, the response of its action to a change in the metric is what

963
00:51:48,364 --> 00:51:51,302
defines the stress–energy tensor.

964
00:51:51,302 --> 00:51:55,489
The total action is stationary for every possible variation of the metric

965
00:51:55,489 --> 00:51:57,589
only if the bracket vanishes:

966
00:51:57,589 --> 00:52:02,252
G mu nu equals eight pi G over c to the fourth times T mu nu.

967
00:52:02,252 --> 00:52:06,464
The same equation, from one line of input.

968
00:52:06,631 --> 00:52:08,956
Two routes, one destination.

969
00:52:08,956 --> 00:52:13,968
And in 1971, David Lovelock proved that this isn't a coincidence.

970
00:52:13,968 --> 00:52:18,581
In four dimensions, the only symmetric, divergence-free tensors you can build

971
00:52:18,581 --> 00:52:22,656
from the metric and its first two derivatives are combinations of the Einstein

972
00:52:22,656 --> 00:52:25,093
tensor and the metric itself.

973
00:52:25,093 --> 00:52:29,456
So once you accept the requirements, Einstein's equation isn't a guess.

974
00:52:29,456 --> 00:52:31,756
It's the only possibility.

975
00:52:31,756 --> 00:52:35,393
Even energy conservation comes for free from the action:

976
00:52:35,393 --> 00:52:39,356
it follows from the fact that the action doesn't care about coordinates.

977
00:52:39,356 --> 00:52:40,793
As for priority:

978
00:52:40,793 --> 00:52:44,793
Hilbert submitted his variational paper on the twentieth of November;

979
00:52:44,793 --> 00:52:48,068
Einstein presented his equations on the twenty-fifth.

980
00:52:48,068 --> 00:52:52,406
The physical ideas that led there were Einstein's, built over eight years.

981
00:52:57,663 --> 00:53:01,838
Einstein's equation is ten coupled nonlinear partial differential equations,

982
00:53:01,838 --> 00:53:05,488
and Einstein himself had worked only with approximate solutions.

983
00:53:05,488 --> 00:53:09,650
The first exact one came within weeks, and its simplicity surprised him.

984
00:53:09,650 --> 00:53:11,413
Take the simplest situation:

985
00:53:11,413 --> 00:53:14,425
empty space outside a static, spherical star.

986
00:53:14,425 --> 00:53:18,363
There, T is zero, so the trace-reversed equation says simply that the Ricci

987
00:53:18,363 --> 00:53:19,913
tensor vanishes.

988
00:53:19,913 --> 00:53:22,838
Symmetry narrows down the metric enormously.

989
00:53:22,838 --> 00:53:25,763
Static means nothing depends on time.

990
00:53:25,763 --> 00:53:30,238
Spherical means the angular part is r squared times the metric of a sphere,

991
00:53:30,238 --> 00:53:35,200
with r defined so that the sphere at r has area four pi r squared.

992
00:53:35,200 --> 00:53:38,300
All that's left unknown are two functions of r:

993
00:53:38,300 --> 00:53:43,013
alpha in the time part, and beta in the radial part.

994
00:53:43,213 --> 00:53:46,988
Now it's just computation, the same machinery we've built, applied to this

995
00:53:46,988 --> 00:53:47,963
metric.

996
00:53:47,963 --> 00:53:49,888
First the Christoffel symbols:

997
00:53:49,888 --> 00:53:52,050
nine kinds are nonzero.

998
00:53:52,050 --> 00:53:56,475
Then the Ricci tensor, through the formula for Riemann and one contraction.

999
00:53:56,475 --> 00:54:00,288
Everything off the diagonal vanishes, and the four diagonal components are

1000
00:54:00,288 --> 00:54:01,300
these.

1001
00:54:01,300 --> 00:54:07,213
They look complicated, but setting them to zero is surprisingly easy.

1002
00:54:07,396 --> 00:54:11,596
Add the first two, with the right factor, and every second derivative cancels,

1003
00:54:11,596 --> 00:54:15,033
leaving two over r times alpha prime plus beta prime.

1004
00:54:15,033 --> 00:54:17,121
So beta is minus alpha.

1005
00:54:17,121 --> 00:54:20,883
Substitute that into the angular equation, and it becomes the derivative of

1006
00:54:20,883 --> 00:54:24,633
r times e to the two alpha, which must equal one.

1007
00:54:24,633 --> 00:54:25,633
Integrate:

1008
00:54:25,633 --> 00:54:29,708
r e to the two alpha is r minus a constant, which we'll call r s.

1009
00:54:29,708 --> 00:54:34,771
So e to the two alpha is one minus r s over r.

1010
00:54:34,904 --> 00:54:36,092
What is r s?

1011
00:54:36,092 --> 00:54:39,679
Far away, the time part of the metric has to match the weak-field form we

1012
00:54:39,679 --> 00:54:41,117
found from clocks:

1013
00:54:41,117 --> 00:54:45,954
minus one plus two phi over c squared, with phi equal to minus G M over r.

1014
00:54:45,954 --> 00:54:49,329
So r s is two G M over c squared.

1015
00:54:49,329 --> 00:54:53,479
This is the Schwarzschild solution, found by Karl Schwarzschild in December

1016
00:54:53,479 --> 00:54:58,329
1915, while he was serving as an artillery officer on the Russian front.

1017
00:54:58,329 --> 00:55:00,429
He died the following May.

1018
00:55:00,429 --> 00:55:04,554
And by Birkhoff's theorem, it's the only spherically symmetric solution in

1019
00:55:04,554 --> 00:55:05,817
empty space:

1020
00:55:05,817 --> 00:55:10,829
even a pulsating star has exactly this field outside it.

1021
00:55:10,971 --> 00:55:12,396
How big is r s?

1022
00:55:12,396 --> 00:55:16,621
For the Sun, about three kilometers, buried deep inside a star seven hundred

1023
00:55:16,621 --> 00:55:18,821
thousand kilometers in radius.

1024
00:55:18,821 --> 00:55:21,071
For the Earth, nine millimeters.

1025
00:55:21,071 --> 00:55:24,008
So for ordinary bodies, the corrections are tiny:

1026
00:55:24,008 --> 00:55:28,771
a clock on the Earth's surface runs slow, relative to one far out in space,

1027
00:55:28,771 --> 00:55:34,371
by seven parts in ten billion, about sixty microseconds a day.

1028
00:55:34,479 --> 00:55:36,942
But the solution itself goes much further.

1029
00:55:36,942 --> 00:55:39,804
Here's the rate of a clock at rest, as a function of r.

1030
00:55:39,804 --> 00:55:41,417
Far away it's one.

1031
00:55:41,417 --> 00:55:46,867
Closer in, it slows, and at r equals r s, it stops altogether, as seen from

1032
00:55:46,867 --> 00:55:48,054
far away.

1033
00:55:48,054 --> 00:55:51,304
Something strange happens there.

1034
00:55:51,438 --> 00:55:54,850
Space is curved too, and we can picture it, with care.

1035
00:55:54,850 --> 00:55:58,863
Take a slice through the equatorial plane, at one moment of time.

1036
00:55:58,863 --> 00:56:03,188
Circles around the center still have circumference two pi r, but the radial

1037
00:56:03,188 --> 00:56:07,238
distance between neighboring circles is stretched by one over the square root

1038
00:56:07,238 --> 00:56:09,650
of one minus r s over r.

1039
00:56:09,650 --> 00:56:14,363
A two-dimensional surface with exactly those distances is this funnel, Flamm's

1040
00:56:14,363 --> 00:56:15,863
paraboloid.

1041
00:56:15,863 --> 00:56:19,913
Unlike the rubber sheet, it's an honest picture of the geometry of space,

1042
00:56:19,913 --> 00:56:21,700
and nothing rolls down it:

1043
00:56:21,700 --> 00:56:26,113
it only shows that, near r s, there's more room between the circles than flat

1044
00:56:26,113 --> 00:56:28,713
space allows.

1045
00:56:28,871 --> 00:56:30,221
Here's what's strange.

1046
00:56:30,221 --> 00:56:32,858
Draw the paths of light rays, in these coordinates.

1047
00:56:32,858 --> 00:56:36,433
Far away, light cones have their usual forty-five degree shape.

1048
00:56:36,433 --> 00:56:39,883
Closer in, they narrow, and at r s they close up completely:

1049
00:56:39,883 --> 00:56:42,996
as seen from far away, light there can't make progress outward.

1050
00:56:42,996 --> 00:56:45,383
Is spacetime itself broken at r s?

1051
00:56:45,383 --> 00:56:46,146
No.

1052
00:56:46,146 --> 00:56:49,771
The curvature, measured by an invariant like this one, built from Riemann,

1053
00:56:49,771 --> 00:56:51,621
is perfectly finite there.

1054
00:56:51,621 --> 00:56:55,746
It's the coordinates that fail, like longitude at the North Pole.

1055
00:56:55,746 --> 00:57:00,721
The real singularity is at r equals zero, where curvature becomes infinite.

1056
00:57:00,721 --> 00:57:03,333
But r s is real in another sense:

1057
00:57:03,333 --> 00:57:07,746
in better coordinates, you find that inside it, every future direction points

1058
00:57:07,746 --> 00:57:08,721
inward.

1059
00:57:08,721 --> 00:57:11,146
Nothing that crosses it can come back out.

1060
00:57:11,146 --> 00:57:13,496
It's the event horizon of a black hole.

1061
00:57:15,185 --> 00:57:18,810
Now let's put the Schwarzschild solution to work, starting with the puzzle

1062
00:57:18,810 --> 00:57:20,073
we began with.

1063
00:57:20,073 --> 00:57:24,435
In Newton's theory, a single planet around a star traces a closed ellipse

1064
00:57:24,435 --> 00:57:25,485
forever.

1065
00:57:25,485 --> 00:57:28,023
Mercury's ellipse slowly turns.

1066
00:57:28,023 --> 00:57:32,110
After accounting for the pull of every other planet, there remains a turning

1067
00:57:32,110 --> 00:57:35,960
of forty-three arcseconds per century.

1068
00:57:36,635 --> 00:57:40,198
A planet follows a geodesic of the Schwarzschild metric.

1069
00:57:40,198 --> 00:57:44,335
Write the geodesic Lagrangian for an orbit in the equatorial plane.

1070
00:57:44,335 --> 00:57:49,048
The metric doesn't depend on t, so the quantity conjugate to t is conserved:

1071
00:57:49,048 --> 00:57:50,510
that's the energy.

1072
00:57:50,510 --> 00:57:54,960
It doesn't depend on phi either, so the angular momentum is conserved.

1073
00:57:54,960 --> 00:57:59,248
Use these, together with the condition that the four-velocity has length c,

1074
00:57:59,248 --> 00:58:02,235
to eliminate t dot and phi dot.

1075
00:58:02,235 --> 00:58:06,185
What's left looks exactly like a particle rolling in one dimension, in an

1076
00:58:06,185 --> 00:58:07,810
effective potential.

1077
00:58:07,810 --> 00:58:09,923
Its first two terms are Newton's:

1078
00:58:09,923 --> 00:58:12,448
gravity and the centrifugal barrier.

1079
00:58:12,448 --> 00:58:13,785
The third is new:

1080
00:58:13,785 --> 00:58:19,010
an extra attraction, falling off like one over r cubed.

1081
00:58:19,168 --> 00:58:20,843
Here are the two potentials.

1082
00:58:20,843 --> 00:58:23,306
Newton's has an infinite centrifugal wall:

1083
00:58:23,306 --> 00:58:26,481
no planet with angular momentum can reach the center.

1084
00:58:26,481 --> 00:58:30,518
Einstein's follows Newton's far away, but close in, the new term wins, the

1085
00:58:30,518 --> 00:58:33,231
wall turns over, and the potential plunges.

1086
00:58:33,231 --> 00:58:37,393
A bound orbit still rocks back and forth between a nearest and farthest point.

1087
00:58:37,393 --> 00:58:40,381
But close to the hole, something Newton never allowed:

1088
00:58:40,381 --> 00:58:45,131
inside three Schwarzschild radii, no circular orbit is stable, and a particle

1089
00:58:45,131 --> 00:58:48,668
with enough energy simply falls in.

1090
00:58:48,802 --> 00:58:51,602
To get the shape of the orbit, change variables.

1091
00:58:51,602 --> 00:58:53,677
Start from the energy equation.

1092
00:58:53,677 --> 00:58:58,702
Use u, one over r, as the variable, and the angle phi instead of time:

1093
00:58:58,702 --> 00:59:04,489
by conservation of angular momentum, r dot is minus l times d u d phi.

1094
00:59:04,489 --> 00:59:08,977
Differentiate once more with respect to phi, and the constant disappears.

1095
00:59:08,977 --> 00:59:12,243
This is the relativistic orbit equation.

1096
00:59:12,243 --> 00:59:14,606
Without the last term, it's Newton's:

1097
00:59:14,606 --> 00:59:18,781
its solution is a conic section, an ellipse for a bound planet.

1098
00:59:18,781 --> 00:59:24,131
The last term is Einstein's correction, three G M u squared over c squared.

1099
00:59:24,131 --> 00:59:29,368
For Mercury it's about ten million times smaller than the others.

1100
00:59:29,527 --> 00:59:31,314
Small as it is, it adds up.

1101
00:59:31,314 --> 00:59:34,502
Start from Newton's ellipse, and feed it into the correction term.

1102
00:59:34,502 --> 00:59:38,502
Squaring gives a constant, a cosine of phi, and a cosine squared.

1103
00:59:38,502 --> 00:59:40,414
The middle term is the troublemaker:

1104
00:59:40,414 --> 00:59:44,677
it pushes the orbit at exactly its own natural frequency, like pushing a swing

1105
00:59:44,677 --> 00:59:46,302
in time with its motion.

1106
00:59:46,302 --> 00:59:48,527
Its effect grows with every orbit.

1107
00:59:48,527 --> 00:59:51,977
The result is an ellipse whose angle runs slightly slow:

1108
00:59:51,977 --> 00:59:56,977
the planet returns to its closest point not after two pi, but a little later.

1109
00:59:56,977 --> 01:00:01,777
Per orbit, the ellipse turns by six pi G M over c squared, a, one minus e

1110
01:00:01,777 --> 01:00:04,002
squared.

1111
01:00:04,135 --> 01:00:05,310
Now the numbers.

1112
01:00:05,310 --> 01:00:09,623
For Mercury, the semi-major axis, eccentricity, and the mass of the Sun give

1113
01:00:09,623 --> 01:00:13,173
a turn of five times ten to the minus seven radians per orbit:

1114
01:00:13,173 --> 01:00:14,748
a tenth of an arcsecond.

1115
01:00:14,748 --> 01:00:17,948
Mercury orbits four hundred and fifteen times a century.

1116
01:00:17,948 --> 01:00:18,673
Total:

1117
01:00:18,673 --> 01:00:21,673
forty-two point nine eight arcseconds per century.

1118
01:00:21,673 --> 01:00:25,548
Exactly the anomaly astronomers had been unable to explain for more than half

1119
01:00:25,548 --> 01:00:26,698
a century.

1120
01:00:26,698 --> 01:00:31,485
Einstein found this on the eighteenth of November, 1915, with no adjustable

1121
01:00:31,485 --> 01:00:32,985
parameters.

1122
01:00:32,985 --> 01:00:37,123
He later wrote to a friend that for several days he was beside himself with

1123
01:00:37,123 --> 01:00:39,260
joy.

1124
01:00:39,402 --> 01:00:43,664
Here's that effect, magnified about six hundred thousand times, by integrating

1125
01:00:43,664 --> 01:00:47,014
the exact orbit equation around a far denser star.

1126
01:00:47,014 --> 01:00:50,764
Each time the planet swings past its closest point, the ellipse has turned

1127
01:00:50,764 --> 01:00:53,202
a little further, tracing out a rosette.

1128
01:00:53,202 --> 01:00:56,552
For the real Mercury, forty-three arcseconds is tiny:

1129
01:00:56,552 --> 01:01:00,052
lay it next to the full Moon, and it's a forty-third of the Moon's width,

1130
01:01:00,052 --> 01:01:02,139
accumulated over a century.

1131
01:01:02,139 --> 01:01:05,602
Astronomers measured it anyway, and Newton couldn't account for it.

1132
01:01:07,816 --> 01:01:09,754
Light follows geodesics too:

1133
01:01:09,754 --> 01:01:13,154
null geodesics, along which the interval is zero.

1134
01:01:13,154 --> 01:01:17,341
The same derivation as for planets gives the orbit equation without the Newtonian

1135
01:01:17,341 --> 01:01:20,191
term, because light has no rest mass.

1136
01:01:20,191 --> 01:01:24,391
Without the right-hand side, the solution is a straight line passing at distance

1137
01:01:24,391 --> 01:01:26,629
b, the impact parameter.

1138
01:01:26,629 --> 01:01:29,541
Put that line into the small term and solve again:

1139
01:01:29,541 --> 01:01:33,291
the ray picks up a correction that bends it toward the mass.

1140
01:01:33,291 --> 01:01:35,691
Far from the mass, u goes to zero.

1141
01:01:35,691 --> 01:01:38,666
Without bending, that happens at phi equals zero;

1142
01:01:38,666 --> 01:01:42,329
with it, at a small negative angle, half the deflection.

1143
01:01:42,329 --> 01:01:43,216
Solving:

1144
01:01:43,216 --> 01:01:48,516
the total deflection is four G M over c squared b.

1145
01:01:48,633 --> 01:01:52,458
For a ray grazing the Sun, that's one point seven five arcseconds.

1146
01:01:52,458 --> 01:01:54,445
And here's a beautiful detail.

1147
01:01:54,445 --> 01:01:58,233
Half of that comes from the warping of time, the part we found with clocks.

1148
01:01:58,233 --> 01:02:00,745
The other half comes from the warping of space.

1149
01:02:00,745 --> 01:02:05,283
In 1911, using only the equivalence principle, Einstein predicted the first

1150
01:02:05,283 --> 01:02:06,345
half alone:

1151
01:02:06,345 --> 01:02:10,320
about point eight arcseconds, the same as Newtonian physics would give for

1152
01:02:10,320 --> 01:02:12,770
a particle moving at the speed of light.

1153
01:02:12,770 --> 01:02:16,008
Slow objects feel only the warping of time.

1154
01:02:16,008 --> 01:02:20,170
Light moves as fast through space as through time, and feels both.

1155
01:02:20,170 --> 01:02:24,858
So measuring light bending tests whether space is curved.

1156
01:02:25,024 --> 01:02:28,912
To see starlight grazing the Sun, you need the Sun's light blocked:

1157
01:02:28,912 --> 01:02:30,449
a total eclipse.

1158
01:02:30,449 --> 01:02:34,562
Photograph the stars around the eclipsed Sun, and compare with photographs

1159
01:02:34,562 --> 01:02:37,537
of the same stars at night, months later.

1160
01:02:37,537 --> 01:02:42,124
Each star should appear pushed away from the Sun, by an amount inversely proportional

1161
01:02:42,124 --> 01:02:44,049
to its distance from it.

1162
01:02:44,049 --> 01:02:49,824
In 1919, two British expeditions, to Sobral in Brazil and the island of Príncipe

1163
01:02:49,824 --> 01:02:54,674
off Africa, measured one point nine eight and one point six one arcseconds,

1164
01:02:54,674 --> 01:02:57,012
with uncertainties of a few tenths.

1165
01:02:57,012 --> 01:03:01,712
The announcement in London that November made Einstein world famous overnight.

1166
01:03:01,712 --> 01:03:08,174
Today, radio telescopes confirm the prediction to one part in ten thousand.

1167
01:03:08,358 --> 01:03:12,070
Now go close to a black hole, where the bending is no longer small.

1168
01:03:12,070 --> 01:03:15,970
Here are exact light paths, computed from the geodesic equation, all coming

1169
01:03:15,970 --> 01:03:17,183
in from the left.

1170
01:03:17,183 --> 01:03:18,845
Far out, they bend a little.

1171
01:03:18,845 --> 01:03:22,208
Closer in, they swing around the hole, and at one and a half Schwarzschild

1172
01:03:22,208 --> 01:03:24,558
radii, light can travel in a circle:

1173
01:03:24,558 --> 01:03:26,070
the photon sphere.

1174
01:03:26,070 --> 01:03:29,758
Any ray aimed within about two point six Schwarzschild radii of the center

1175
01:03:29,758 --> 01:03:30,795
falls in.

1176
01:03:30,795 --> 01:03:34,395
So a black hole casts a shadow more than two and a half times the size of

1177
01:03:34,395 --> 01:03:36,808
its horizon.

1178
01:03:36,916 --> 01:03:40,604
Put a disk of hot gas around the hole, and trace light from every pixel of

1179
01:03:40,604 --> 01:03:43,429
a camera back along those same exact paths.

1180
01:03:43,429 --> 01:03:46,566
This is what you'd see, from slightly above the disk.

1181
01:03:46,566 --> 01:03:49,329
The far side of the disk isn't hidden behind the hole:

1182
01:03:49,329 --> 01:03:52,629
its light bends up and over the top, and under the bottom.

1183
01:03:52,629 --> 01:03:56,566
The gas moves at a sizable fraction of the speed of light, so the side coming

1184
01:03:56,566 --> 01:03:59,341
toward us is brightened by the Doppler effect.

1185
01:03:59,341 --> 01:04:02,654
And the thin rings close to the shadow are light that went partway around

1186
01:04:02,654 --> 01:04:05,741
the hole before escaping.

1187
01:04:06,458 --> 01:04:11,033
Seen more nearly face-on, the disk surrounds a dark shadow, edged by a bright

1188
01:04:11,033 --> 01:04:12,095
ring.

1189
01:04:12,095 --> 01:04:17,395
In 2019, the Event Horizon Telescope, a network of radio dishes the size of

1190
01:04:17,395 --> 01:04:21,295
the Earth, photographed exactly this around the black hole at the center of

1191
01:04:21,295 --> 01:04:26,858
the galaxy M87, six and a half billion times the mass of the Sun, and three

1192
01:04:26,858 --> 01:04:30,208
years later the one at the center of our own galaxy.

1193
01:04:32,364 --> 01:04:34,752
Remember the problem with Newton's gravity:

1194
01:04:34,752 --> 01:04:36,402
it acts instantly.

1195
01:04:36,402 --> 01:04:38,277
Does Einstein's theory fix that?

1196
01:04:38,277 --> 01:04:39,714
Look at weak gravity:

1197
01:04:39,714 --> 01:04:44,002
the metric is the flat one plus a small ripple, h, and we keep only terms

1198
01:04:44,002 --> 01:04:44,839
linear in h.

1199
01:04:44,839 --> 01:04:48,639
There's freedom in choosing coordinates, and a clever choice, applied to the

1200
01:04:48,639 --> 01:04:52,227
trace-reversed ripple, simplifies things enormously.

1201
01:04:52,227 --> 01:04:57,052
The Einstein tensor becomes just minus one half the wave operator, box, acting

1202
01:04:57,052 --> 01:04:58,697
on h bar.

1203
01:04:58,697 --> 01:05:03,922
So Einstein's equation becomes box h bar equals minus sixteen pi G over c

1204
01:05:03,922 --> 01:05:05,285
to the fourth times T.

1205
01:05:05,285 --> 01:05:08,547
And in empty space, that's the wave equation.

1206
01:05:08,547 --> 01:05:13,072
Ripples in the metric travel, and they travel at exactly the speed of light.

1207
01:05:13,072 --> 01:05:16,922
Gravity has caught up with relativity.

1208
01:05:17,006 --> 01:05:18,931
What does a gravitational wave do?

1209
01:05:18,931 --> 01:05:22,831
With a further choice of coordinates, a wave moving along z is described by

1210
01:05:22,831 --> 01:05:24,093
just two numbers:

1211
01:05:24,093 --> 01:05:27,206
two polarizations, called plus and cross.

1212
01:05:27,206 --> 01:05:30,281
Lay out a ring of free particles across its path.

1213
01:05:30,281 --> 01:05:34,631
As the wave passes, the plus polarization stretches the ring along one axis

1214
01:05:34,631 --> 01:05:37,893
while squeezing it along the other, then the reverse.

1215
01:05:37,893 --> 01:05:43,631
The cross polarization does the same, along axes turned by forty-five degrees.

1216
01:05:43,631 --> 01:05:46,043
Shape changes, no volume change:

1217
01:05:46,043 --> 01:05:51,606
this is Weyl curvature, traveling through empty space at the speed of light.

1218
01:05:52,772 --> 01:05:53,897
What makes them?

1219
01:05:53,897 --> 01:05:57,622
Solving the wave equation with a source gives the quadrupole formula:

1220
01:05:57,622 --> 01:06:01,610
the wave is proportional to the second time derivative of the mass distribution's

1221
01:06:01,610 --> 01:06:04,935
quadrupole moment, measured at the retarded time.

1222
01:06:04,935 --> 01:06:06,847
A spinning dumbbell radiates;

1223
01:06:06,847 --> 01:06:08,922
a pulsating sphere doesn't.

1224
01:06:08,922 --> 01:06:13,247
Roughly, the amplitude is the source's Schwarzschild radius divided by your

1225
01:06:13,247 --> 01:06:17,297
distance from it, times its speed squared over c squared.

1226
01:06:17,297 --> 01:06:21,460
For the most violent events in the universe, two black holes colliding at

1227
01:06:21,460 --> 01:06:25,697
a good fraction of the speed of light, more than a billion light-years away,

1228
01:06:25,697 --> 01:06:29,497
that's about ten to the minus twenty-one.

1229
01:06:29,656 --> 01:06:31,906
Two black holes in orbit radiate.

1230
01:06:31,906 --> 01:06:34,993
The waves carry away energy, so the orbit shrinks;

1231
01:06:34,993 --> 01:06:38,406
a tighter orbit is faster, so it radiates more strongly;

1232
01:06:38,406 --> 01:06:40,543
so it shrinks faster still.

1233
01:06:40,543 --> 01:06:42,143
The result is a chirp:

1234
01:06:42,143 --> 01:06:46,693
a wave whose frequency and amplitude sweep upward until the black holes merge.

1235
01:06:46,693 --> 01:06:50,943
The quadrupole formula predicts the sweep exactly, and it depends on the masses

1236
01:06:50,943 --> 01:06:54,231
through a single combination, the chirp mass.

1237
01:06:54,231 --> 01:06:58,956
For thirty solar masses, the last stretch, from thirty-five hertz to the merger,

1238
01:06:58,956 --> 01:07:02,206
takes about a sixth of a second.

1239
01:07:02,414 --> 01:07:04,439
Measuring that seemed hopeless.

1240
01:07:04,439 --> 01:07:09,502
Then, on the fourteenth of September 2015, the two detectors of LIGO, in Washington

1241
01:07:09,502 --> 01:07:14,164
State and Louisiana, three thousand kilometers apart, recorded this.

1242
01:07:14,164 --> 01:07:17,614
This is the actual data, which LIGO makes public.

1243
01:07:17,614 --> 01:07:21,964
Livingston saw the wave seven milliseconds before Hanford, within the ten

1244
01:07:21,964 --> 01:07:25,852
milliseconds that light needs to travel between them, and flipped, because

1245
01:07:25,852 --> 01:07:28,231
its arms point differently.

1246
01:07:28,231 --> 01:07:32,218
Spread the signal out by frequency, and the chirp is unmistakable:

1247
01:07:32,218 --> 01:07:36,081
a rising sweep, from about thirty-five hertz to a few hundred.

1248
01:07:36,081 --> 01:07:39,831
And laid over it, the sweep that the quadrupole formula predicts for a chirp

1249
01:07:39,831 --> 01:07:41,893
mass of thirty-one Suns.

1250
01:07:41,893 --> 01:07:46,418
Two black holes, thirty-six and thirty-one times the mass of the Sun, merged

1251
01:07:46,418 --> 01:07:48,131
into one of sixty-three.

1252
01:07:48,131 --> 01:07:52,431
The missing three solar masses left as gravitational waves.

1253
01:07:52,431 --> 01:07:56,593
By the time they reached Earth, they stretched LIGO's four kilometer arms

1254
01:07:56,593 --> 01:07:59,006
by a four hundredth of the width of a proton.

1255
01:08:00,687 --> 01:08:02,187
Let's put it all together.

1256
01:08:02,187 --> 01:08:04,275
It started with a falling elevator:

1257
01:08:04,275 --> 01:08:07,725
in free fall, gravity disappears, at least locally.

1258
01:08:07,725 --> 01:08:10,437
So gravity can't be an ordinary force.

1259
01:08:10,437 --> 01:08:14,650
It has to be part of the geometry of spacetime, and its slow-motion part lives

1260
01:08:14,650 --> 01:08:18,087
in the rate of clocks, in the time component of the metric.

1261
01:08:18,087 --> 01:08:22,837
Free objects follow the paths of maximal proper time, and for slow objects,

1262
01:08:22,837 --> 01:08:26,404
the geodesic equation reproduces Newton's law.

1263
01:08:26,404 --> 01:08:28,666
What falling can't erase is tides:

1264
01:08:28,666 --> 01:08:31,866
neighboring free paths converging and spreading apart.

1265
01:08:31,866 --> 01:08:35,154
That's curvature, measured by the Riemann tensor.

1266
01:08:35,154 --> 01:08:39,341
Matter enters through the stress–energy tensor, and the requirement that the

1267
01:08:39,341 --> 01:08:44,154
geometry side conserve energy and momentum as automatically as matter does,

1268
01:08:44,154 --> 01:08:48,091
together with Newton's limit, leaves exactly one equation.

1269
01:08:48,091 --> 01:08:52,779
Hilbert's action gives the same one, and Lovelock showed there's no other.

1270
01:08:52,779 --> 01:08:57,141
Its first solution explained Mercury and predicted the bending of light, and

1271
01:08:57,141 --> 01:09:01,979
it went on to predict black holes, and waves in spacetime itself, a hundred

1272
01:09:01,979 --> 01:09:05,329
years before they were observed.

1273
01:09:05,495 --> 01:09:07,433
Ten equations, one line.

1274
01:09:07,433 --> 01:09:09,545
Spacetime tells matter how to move;

1275
01:09:09,545 --> 01:09:11,733
matter tells spacetime how to curve.

1276
01:09:11,733 --> 01:09:12,920
Thanks for watching.
